window.harnessChunks["rows-5475071e0a92327c.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T17:19:38+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensesh/KARIMO\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">KARIMO</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensesh/KARIMO/tree/305abbe8d7a86d01023913cd12072cd83e937f52\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 305abbe8d7a86d01023913cd12072cd83e937f52\">305abbe &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Research codebase/external technical context, prepare task briefs and implement/test/document concrete feature changes in an actual project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Source/API uncertainty, contradictory requirements, failing tests, changing files, dependencies and task-specific errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Installed model-backed researcher, interviewer, implementer, tester and documenter roles choose project inspections, edits, tests and corrections within assigned briefs; outputs return as research artifacts, commits, test evidence and PR results. - Decision owner: Selected first-party KARIMO agent role operating on the Claude Code Task/tool host, not Bash alone or the generic Claude runtime acting without the role configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent frontmatter and tools, file-boundary rules, worker worktrees, task brief, gh, git, tests, status and CI. - Closure path: User feature request → research/PRD/brief → PM&#39;s task assignment → model worker reads actual code and chooses write/check actions → source/test/commit/PR result → PM and ultimately user. - Boundary reachability: The marketplace declares the plugin and /karimo:run explicitly spawns PM and worker types. Implementer has Read/Write/Edit/Bash tools, source-inspection instructions, validation and commit/PR handoff. External generic Claude Code cognition is not claimed as an independent KARIMO operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing model-backed workers leaves install/manifest/branch scripts and static instructions but no task-specific product research, code design, chosen edits or semantic repair. - Evidence: manifest and plugin declaration; run&#39;s installed spawn path; worker task/tool/validation contract; worker implementation and test return; research role and technical source access. - Basis: First-party installed model/tool agent configurations linked through the normal task workflow. - Confidence: High for specified operational ownership path; no generated-code-quality measurement. - Caveats: Model, host and GitHub permissions must exist, and prompt-obedience is not verified by native shell tests. A feature may be run with fewer roles.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Pre-empt a concrete conflict between distinct planned workers whose briefs encode incompatible wave dependencies, overlapping files or mutually inconsistent acceptance criteria, so their subsequent execution instructions no longer collide. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two tasks placed in a parallel wave both modifying dependent/shared files, one worker expecting a file a later-wave worker must first create, or conflicting per-wave success criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party brief-reviewer inspects the cross-task briefs against actual source/dependency state and proposes correction; brief-corrector can modify task dependencies, briefs and wave preconditions. In the default run command the human must select the apply-fixes path before those corrections take effect, so autonomous end-to-end S2 attenuation is not established. - Decision owner: A composed brief-reviewer → user-choice → brief-corrector construction; there is no claimed autonomous live inter-worker conflict arbitrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Dependency DAG, generated execution plan, file scopes, source/PRD read access, review findings, brief-corrector, model-backed worker prompts, serial wave gates, native worktree separation. - Closure path: Proposed parallel worker briefs and current code → reviewer spots a specific cross-task collision and produces actionable finding → user selects apply fixes → corrector alters tasks.yaml dependencies or briefs → PM dispatches changed instructions in subsequent execution waves. - Boundary reachability: The standard /karimo:run brief-review phase challenges task order, dependencies and file-boundary collisions, then exposes an explicit correction option wired to a shipped corrector. The positive claim is limited to this selected preparatory mode; --skip-review bypasses it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party source-aware conflict detector and editing agent can settle a specified cross-S1 task interference, but the essential default user choice means a fully autonomous S2 function is not shown. Generic wave ordering, isolated git worktrees, branch guards and PM scheduling alone would not satisfy S2. - Distinct S1 units: Two or more different planned implementer/tester/documenter workers whose briefs share or conflict over concrete source files and prerequisite outputs. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Same-wave edits or inconsistent order/acceptance contracts causing incompatible worker outputs, not generic task uncertainty or review quality. - Attenuating coordination relation: Source-aware cross-brief mismatch finding and explicit rewrite of dependency order / work brief before the named workers are dispatched. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Changed tasks.yaml dependency order and corrected briefs are loaded by PM, then affect worker wave placement and source modifications. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The witness is conflict detection against two named task contracts followed by a concrete correction of their interference, not the existence of a DAG, queue or mailbox. - Evidence: brief-review collision and original-code probes; shared-file sequencing checks; run&#39;s review and correction selection; corrector modifies dependencies and task briefs; deprecation of separate legacy conflict resolver. - Basis: Narrow source-constructed S2-specific pre-dispatch correction, with a necessary user choice; not measured runtime inter-worker reconciliation. - Confidence: Medium-low; preparatory planned-unit conflict correction is a narrower witness than live operational oscillation attenuation. - Caveats: No (P) state is used for S2. A user bypassing review or declining the correction lacks this path. A runtime git merge conflict is not itself credited as resolved by KARIMO; the deprecated review-architect is outside the default feature-mode claim.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Maintain the current PRD&#39;s full task/wave commitments, assign specialized workers, redirect failing tasks and decide current repair/gate/stop actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Dependency stalls, worker failure and incomplete PRs, unexpected code/test/review results, shifting per-task complexity, worktree crashes and exhausted revision budget. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model-backed PM examines current PRD/task status and GitHub/git reality, chooses current worker allocation, wave progress, model escalation, corrective delegation and whether to pause/replan. It spawns pm-reviewer and pm-finalizer for specialized current subflows. Configured deterministic guards can prevent unsafe branch/wave transitions without themselves owning the decisions. - Decision owner: KARIMO karimo-pm role for the one running PRD; user retains product/merge authorization, and native Claude Code/CI only supply execution/check facilities. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tasks.yaml, executionplan.yaml, status.json, findings.md, worktree/branch guards, provider result adapters, wave merge verification, human gate model and git-state crash reconstruction. - Closure path: PRD scope and task graph plus current worker/PR/test/review feedback → PM selects remaining worker/wave/rework/escalation commitment → task/tool dispatch or pause → changed PRD work and task/PR state return for subsequent decisions. - Boundary reachability: /karimo:run invokes this model-backed role in the shipped feature workflow. PM receives a whole-PRD status view and explicit authority over its task delegation, waves, review/repair and current stop/resume choices. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Branch assertions, automatic test gates and concurrency caps are fixed mechanisms; removing the PM model actor removes task-specific current prioritization, rerouting and correction decisions. - Whole-system current view: Current PRD/task graph, briefs, execution plan, model assignment, status.json, GitHub PR merge/review states, findings and open gates across the PRD&#39;s workers. - Current-control decision scope: Which task to execute/retry and with which worker/model, how to respond to review and stalls, what wave/gate can proceed, and whether this single PRD is ready or blocked; not global multi-project enterprise policy. - Evidence: PM owner and current scope; PRD-wide inputs and overlap check; current plan/collision probes; wave and worker delegation loop; PR/review return; terminal wave gate. - Basis: First-party configured model coordinator with current multi-worker commitment and feedback paths. - Confidence: Medium; source-wiring evidence, not observed live model control effectiveness. - Caveats: Initial user confirmation and optional human wave gates constrain execution. The role&#39;s embedded Bash snippets are instructions, not a verified standalone PM scheduler runtime. automergeongreen claims are deployment-dependent and do not establish an independent ultimate authority.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Challenge the preceding research/planning/brief agents&#39; readiness claims against the actual codebase and task requirements before executable commitments are handed to workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A brief writer&#39;s incorrect file assumptions, infeasible success criteria, missing prerequisite, contradictory tasks and project configuration mismatch not visible in a summary-only status signal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Separately configured brief-reviewer uses Read/Grep/Glob/Bash to directly inspect first-party project files and distinguishes critical blockers from warnings, returning HOLD/clearance findings. The run command makes those findings part of the human-gated correction path and can dispatch a different brief-corrector before current work begins. - Decision owner: The model-backed KARIMO brief-reviewer for substantive pre-execution audit; it does not itself implement changes. The user/runner determines whether fixes are applied. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate named agent and review step, direct repository artifact access, original PRD/brief inputs, structured findings, correction instructions and optional model-backed corrector. - Closure path: Brief writer&#39;s planned-task claims → independently invoked reviewer checks originals against codebase and cross-task ground truth → documented critical findings and execution clearance → run controller/user routes correction → amended briefs/PRD and ensuing PM task dispatch. - Boundary reachability: The normal /karimo:run flow explicitly spawns a distinct source-reading review role, saves recommendations and exposes corrections before the PM&#39;s execution stage. This is first-party audit of planned work, not first-party independent code audit of every produced PR. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model reviewer selects discrepancies and operational clearance from source evidence independently of brief author reports. A deterministic YAML validator or GitHub PR approval alone would not. Role/context separation is structural, not a proof of separate vendor, host isolation or actual quality. - Claim being audited: Research/brief-generation claim that task contracts reflect current files, dependencies and viable acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: Researcher and brief writer submit consolidated findings, proposed task briefs and dependencies to /karimo:run/PM. - Complementary access path: Reviewer directly reads source code, config files and actual task briefs through its own Read/Grep/Glob/Bash tools, with explicit no-edit/no-commit rule. - Independence boundary: Different model-backed reviewer role/context with read-only instructions and its own clearance verdict; both use Claude Code host and may use same model family. - Who acts on findings: Run controller presents recommendations; user may select first-party corrector to alter tasks before PM delegates them. - Evidence: reviewer task/source-access rights; findings and clearance contract; run&#39;s separate audit and correction-return path; corrector&#39;s no-independent-investigation rule. - Basis: Default-connected, independent source-reading audit path for pre-execution planning claims, not optional external PR review. - Confidence: Medium, limited to the preparatory planning phase. - Caveats: The --skip-review option bypasses this audit; no guaranteed independent model/provider or sandbox separation is evidenced. External Greptile/Claude Code Review results routed by pm-reviewer are not credited as KARIMO&#39;s own independent source-review decision.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Would monitor external/future conditions, select changes to the feature organization&#39;s enduring capability and return those adaptations into subsequent current operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future shifts in product needs, platform options or project environment, beyond technical evidence needed for today&#39;s PRD. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supported S4-specific future capability decision owner at this one-PRD recursion. Research examines external libraries for a named current feature; feedback investigates past/internal KARIMO failures after user prompting and applies user-approved learnings to future tasks. - Decision owner: No source-established autonomous future-policy owner for this PRD organization; human product architect chooses future features and rules. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: researcher WebSearch/WebFetch, /karimo:research external technical references, investigator drift checks, /karimo:feedback interviews, feedback auditor, persistent .karimo/learnings and per-PRD config. - Closure path: Feature research returns into present planning, and retrospective feedback may change future rules after user approval; neither is a differentiated external-prospective intelligence → autonomous organizational capability change → operational return at the declared recursion. - Boundary reachability: These are shipped options but their first-party contracts distinguish feature-focused knowledge gathering and after-the-fact human-triggered improvement, not a standing outside-and-then regulator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Web search or retention of a learned rule does not by itself establish anticipatory environment modelling or authority to change the organization&#39;s future capability. - Evidence: feature-scoped research requirement; external best-practice evidence used in PRDs; feedback is user-triggered and approval-gated; retrospective auditor and approval return. - Basis: Negative at the declared one-PRD recursion; nontrivial adjacent retrospective learning acknowledged. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: At a differently scoped long-lived parent organization, the feedback/learned-rule path could warrant dedicated S4 reassessment. No upgrade of the present PRD&#39;s research activity is inferred.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Would adjudicate an identity/ultimate-purpose conflict and return a binding policy decision for the continuing organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicts over the fundamental product purpose, standing authority, legitimacy or ultimate trade-offs beyond today&#39;s requested PRD. - Decisive decision or feedback right: KARIMO exposes requirement interviews, optional human approval gates, PR merge restrictions, branch/file safety rules and configuration choices, but no first-party identity-policy decision path. - Decision owner: Human project owner outside the assessed one-PRD operating organization retains these strategic/authorization rights; PM owns current task allocation, not enduring purpose. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLAUDE/KARIMO rules, PRD approval, gated execution, nevertouch/review boundaries, GitHub merge approval and review-provider configuration. - Closure path: User may authorize, pause, edit or merge the current feature; no evidence of an ultimate identity conflict deliberated and returned as organizational policy to subsequent PRDs. - Boundary reachability: Existing commands implement task acceptance and human limits, not identity-level governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Prompt rules, GitHub branch protection and ordinary operator assent are constraints, not a policy-setting agent or fully specified parent-governed S5 mechanism. - Evidence: run human approval and options; PM current scope; manual/auto merge command permissions. - Basis: Bounded source-negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A parent institution may exercise S5 for the project/company, but that would be another system in focus. No standalone P inferred from ordinary PR approvals.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d38427fb9fe4519340de4ee902fe1b0cd97ad478/assessments/ka/karimo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d38427fb9fe4519340de4ee902fe1b0cd97ad478\">d38427f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T17:08:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zeroclaw-labs/zeroclaw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ZeroClaw</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zeroclaw-labs/zeroclaw/tree/757db6c356c61861256a87222e30c7c92e8f166f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 757db6c356c61861256a87222e30c7c92e8f166f\">757db6c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user- or event-directed work through a model-driven tool/observation loop and return operational outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous requests, external/tool results, provider responses, failures, installed capabilities and changing task context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive next actions/tools and revise the plan from returned observations within configured authority. - Decision owner: the running ZeroClaw model agent; delegated configured agents own their delegated work at their recursion level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent loop, tool registry/dispatcher, security policy, approval bridge, provider transport, memory/context handling and tool receipts. - Closure path: inbound work → model decision → authorized tool/delegation action → observation/result → subsequent model decision → outcome. - Boundary reachability: the standard zeroclaw agent -a &lt;alias&gt;/channel runtime enters the first-party agent loop shipped in the ZeroClaw binary; no downstream harness is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime code transports and constrains actions, while the model chooses substantive operational actions from current evidence. - Evidence: README.md, crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/agent/loop.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external model inference is execution substrate, not an imported organizational owner.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path was established that attenuates a concrete cross-S1 interference condition among distinct operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S2 at the reviewed boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegation policy, shared action/cost ceilings, depth limits, routing, task sequencing and parallel execution exist, but none is credited without a demonstrated S2-specific disturbance relation. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the repository supports communication and work distribution among agents, but delegation/parallelism alone does not prove coordination in the VSM sense. - Evidence: docs/book/src/agents/delegation.md, crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/delegate.rs. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: reassess if first-party evidence explicitly connects a coordination mechanism to shared-resource contention, oscillation or another cross-S1 disturbance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current portfolio of background work delegated by a configured caller agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing workload, long-running delegated tasks, completed/failed work and tasks that are no longer needed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create background delegated commitments, inspect/list caller-owned tasks, wait for selected tasks and cancel current work. - Decision owner: the model-driven caller agent using the built-in delegate control surface. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: control-plane database, caller-scoped task/result registry, cancellation tokens and delegation policy. - Closure path: current delegated-task portfolio → model invokes list/check/await → current-control decision → delegate/cancel/continue → lifecycle state changes → updated portfolio. - Boundary reachability: delegate is a first-party runtime tool; background mode returns taskid, and the same surface exposes checkresult, listresults, canceltask and awaitsessions for caller-owned tasks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistence/cancellation mechanics are deterministic, while the model caller decides which current commitments to create, inspect, await or cancel. - Evidence: docs/book/src/agents/delegation.md, crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/delegate.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is caller-scoped portfolio control, not a claim that one agent controls every configured task in the installation. - Whole-system current view: within the declared caller-owned delegated-work subsystem, listresults/checkresult expose current lifecycle state and awaitsessions aggregates multiple tasks. - Current-control decision scope: start new delegated work or cancel existing caller-owned background work, changing the current portfolio.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime path establishes an independent complementary auditor whose distinct operational-reality findings close back into corrective S1 behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S3 at the reviewed boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOP audit logging, security/audit records, observability, task recovery and the skill-review fork serve logging/enforcement/recovery or prospective adaptation rather than S3. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: audit loggers store evidence; deterministic monitors enforce/recover state; the model-driven skill reviewer changes future skills and is classified as S4. - Evidence: crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/sop/audit.rs, crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/skills/review.rs. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: evidence stores may feed an external/parent audit process, but no such closing runtime actor was established inside the boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: detect durable lessons from completed operational interaction and modify persistent skills that shape future agent behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user corrections, workflow corrections, failed/outdated skills, reusable techniques/workarounds and newly learned task-class knowledge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether a durable adaptation is warranted and directly patch, extend or archive the installed skill library. - Decision owner: the first-party background SKILL REVIEW model agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: post-turn trigger threshold, failure-signal extraction, restricted review tools, skill validation, atomic writes, cooldowns and audit metadata. - Closure path: completed operational history/tool outcomes → review fork → model identifies durable adaptation → skillmanage mutates persistent skills → later sessions consume changed skills → future operation changes. - Boundary reachability: the review subsystem ships in zeroclaw-runtime; review.rs identifies the production caller path, and enabling first-party SkillImprovementConfig requires no downstream review implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: trigger/write validation are deterministic, but the model review agent decides whether/what durable lesson to encode and can execute the mutation with approval: None. - Evidence: crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/skills/review.rs, crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/skills/reviewprompt.md, crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/skillmanage.rs, crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/skills/improver.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is a configuration-gated shipped operating mode; A does not imply every installation enables it by default. - External distinction: the review fork evaluates evidence from the just-completed operation and user/environment feedback rather than merely continuing the same immediate task loop. - Future / prospective distinction: it decides what durable class-level operating knowledge should govern later sessions, not only how to finish the current turn. - Adaptation option generated: patch an existing skill, add a durable support artifact, create a class-level skill, archive an obsolete skill, or intentionally make no change. - Path back into current capability / S3: accepted review decisions persist directly into the installed skill library; subsequent normal agent turns discover/read those changed skills, so the adapted capability re-enters operational control without a downstream maintainer step.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path establishes organization-level identity/ultimate-policy adjudication closure inside the runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S5 at the reviewed boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configured identity/personality, risk profiles, supervised approvals, security policy and escalatetohuman constrain/escalate current operation but are not by themselves S5. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a human can answer operational questions and authorize actions, but the reviewed path does not resolve organization identity or constitutional-policy questions as an ultimate-policy function. - Evidence: README.md, crates/zeroclaw-tools/src/escalate.rs, crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/identity.rs. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: downstream deployments may place ZeroClaw under a human/external S5 parent; that parent is outside this standalone assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/edbcfbebb4a1d485bbfcc3d232c00f23fd284a21/assessments/zeroclaw.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit edbcfbebb4a1d485bbfcc3d232c00f23fd284a21\">edbcfbe &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T17:46:04+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/greyhaven-ai/autocontext\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">autocontext</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/greyhaven-ai/autocontext/tree/7ccca4681fa113e495c07b0b5b44d28d1181033c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7ccca4681fa113e495c07b0b5b44d28d1181033c\">7ccca46 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform task/scenario attempts and iteratively generate improved strategies/harness artifacts from observed outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: scenario variation, failed actions, changing score and tool/model feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next strategy and propose tools/context mutations after inspecting run feedback. - Decision owner: model-driven competitor/coach/architect roles assembled by the first-party orchestrator, rather than the deterministic runner alone. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scenario executor, validators, model adapters, tournament, persisted run ledger and time/cost bounds. - Closure path: strategy proposal → scenario actions/trials → measured outcome and analysis → subsequent model proposal or halted iteration. - Boundary reachability: the installed autoctx run/solve CLI invokes AgentOrchestrator.rungeneration through stageagentgeneration in the generation pipeline. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model outputs select nonfixed strategies and next actions; runtime gates constrain publication, not the creative operational choice. - Evidence: pinned orchestrator, stages, CLI and generation pipeline above. - Basis: explicit and structurally wired standard distribution. - Confidence: high for the configured model-backed mode. - Caveats: no external provider call or task-success claim was executed in this static assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate competition and conflicting resource claims between simultaneously executing campaign S1 jobs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workers oversubscribing shared concurrency/capacity, duplicate dispatch, stale leases and retry races. - Decisive decision or feedback right: allocate capacity-bounded leases and reservations, reject stale completion, reconcile expired leases, serialize or defer incompatible work. - Decision owner: constructor-defined CampaignScheduler lease/resource algorithm rather than an independently negotiating model coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worker descriptors, global capacity, durable event replay, heartbeat, cancellation and retry state. - Closure path: competing campaign claims → capability/resource reservation and lease assignment → result/timeout/retry recording → subsequent eligible dispatch and changed worker availability. - Boundary reachability: opt-in packaged autoctx campaign run creates the scheduler and drives its lease/dispatch/complete/reconcile paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: coordination choices use authored resource rules; they regulate real simultaneous scenario workers, not just route messages between named roles. - Distinct S1 units: independent scenario/agent campaign jobs capable of model-led task actions. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous jobs can contend for limited worker slots/capabilities and create duplicate or unaccounted external work after lost leases. - Attenuating coordination relation: the central lease ledger and resource/capacity matching prevent overlapping claims and account for stale completions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: completed/expired/canceled leases and retryable failures update available slots and determine whether/where the next S1 job runs. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the reservation/lease protocol materially suppresses shared-resource interference across distinct running S1 cells. - Evidence: pinned campaignscheduler.py and runtime documentation above. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: medium; optional campaign mode. - Caveats: resource allocation is deterministic and no autonomous S2 arbiter is credited.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: regulate current campaign commitments, scarce resources, continuation and deployment of evaluated harness variants. - Disturbance / variety regulated: over-budget runs, stale worker commitments, unacceptable or inconclusive candidate evidence, and failed promotion checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: accept/rollback current generation, allocate/cancel campaign work, and keep incumbent versus activate an evaluated candidate. - Decision owner: constructor-defined budget/gate, scheduler and promotion coordinator; model proposal roles do not own the final integrity/activation rule. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: score/holdout records, candidate digests, run cost trackers, lease ledger, auditor signals and persistence. - Closure path: whole-campaign scores, failures and capacity state → current gate/lease/promotion decision → persisted incumbent/work queue → next generation or dispatch under revised state. - Boundary reachability: the standard generation pipeline invokes gates; optional campaign scheduling and bundle promotion are packaged supported flows with actual coordinator methods. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a proposed strategy alone does not override matched evaluation and promotion policies; final central control remains authored code. - Whole-system current view: active generation results, candidate/incumbent comparisons, campaign lease state, failures, resource limits and budget. - Current-control decision scope: whether present work continues, retries, pauses or rolls back, and whether a validated candidate becomes the current active context. - Evidence: pinned generation pipeline, scheduler and bundle promotion module above. - Basis: structural construction with explicit feedback return. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: no independent managerial actor is inferred from the dashboard or nominal role names.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge ordinary campaign integrity/promotion claims using a differently routed and constrained evidence review. - Disturbance / variety regulated: leaked holdout information, evaluator drift, invalid comparisons, missing reconstruction evidence and unsupported causal assertions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue a cited independent audit finding and severity recommendation on a sealed checkpoint packet. - Decision owner: separately routed model auditor in the explicitly enabled CampaignAuditor mode; deterministic packet guards and policy translation are support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: redacted signed packet builder, distinct endpoint/model-route checks, bounded calls, durable finding store, severity-to-hold policy and operator disposition history. - Closure path: live checkpoint evidence → distinct auditor judgment and cited finding → reviewrequired/safepauserecommended policy outcome → coordinator retains incumbent/holds promotion until disposition or corrective handling. - Boundary reachability: supported AUTOCONTEXTCAMPAIGNAUDITORENABLED with context-bundle promotion or campaign CLI composes the checkpoint runner; ContextBundlePromotionCoordinator tests policy outcomes before activation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: unlike ordinary generator scores or the same-role skeptic, a separately configured model backend receives a restricted independent evidence packet and supplies a distinct judgment; it cannot itself rewrite scores or authorize promotion. - Claim being audited: evidence integrity and promotion readiness of candidate context bundles/campaigns. - Ordinary reporting path: tournament and deterministic gates record scores, comparisons, success/failure and candidate promotion eligibility. - Complementary access path: sealed, allowlisted checkpoint metadata and artifact references constructed outside proposer outputs; the audit model reads this under a distinct route. - Independence boundary: the configured auditor endpoint/model identity must differ from all proposer routes absent an explicit unsafe override; it has read-only access, fixed audit checkpoints and durable separate audit records. - Who acts on findings: first-party promotion policy can hold the incumbent; operator or separately configured controller resolves deferred findings. No claim of independent write or final policy authority. - Evidence: pinned campaign auditor, route checks, campaign auditor document and bundle promotion module above. - Basis: explicit optional audit constructor and live corrective gate; not a claim that every default install runs an independent auditor. - Confidence: medium; opt-in mode and policy configuration required. - Caveats: if route distinction is deliberately overridden or the policy is merely advisory, the positive witness does not apply to that mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: model-authored selection of future context/harness changes responsive to changing task/scenario performance rather than only fixing the current one-off answer. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future task distribution, strategy transfer, scenario performance trends and evolving execution constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: propose new playbook, tool, role-DAG or harness mutations from accumulated scenario outcomes and choose an adaptive candidate for future generations. - Decision owner: model-backed analyst/coach/architect roles; the deterministic bundle validator/promoter governs when a proposed capability can become active. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: run history and trajectory, context-bundle store, adaptive matched trials, held-out comparator, mutation parser and promotion gate. - Closure path: external scenario results across generations → model analysis and future-oriented architecture/context proposal → staged candidate evaluation → active bundle change → changed tools/prompts/routing in subsequent operations. - Boundary reachability: stageagentgeneration extracts architect changes and calls proposecontextbundle; the packaged coordinator evaluates/promotes candidates into active scenario context. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: models select content and architecture options; built-in gates do not themselves create those options. This is restricted to prospective reuse across later generations, not a claim of market or whole-enterprise strategy. - External distinction: held-out and matched scenario task outcomes are environmental evidence beyond the present model response and previous prompt text. - Future / prospective distinction: generated context/role/tool mutations are constructed to change later task attempts rather than merely revise the just-completed answer. - Adaptation option generated: a versioned candidate context bundle containing possible playbook, hint, tool, harness and routing changes. - Path back into current capability / S3: promoted bundle becomes the active context selected by the next run; failed candidates leave the incumbent unchanged. - Evidence: pinned generation stage and context-bundle promotion modules above. - Basis: structural positive path in supported multi-generation model mode. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: evidence is for task-distribution adaptation within the declared harness-evolution boundary, not proactive sensing of an external company strategy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party legitimate authority is shown to decide the overall identity or ultimate priority hierarchy of the harness program. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operator-specified purpose, model credentials, permitted tools, resource/risk constraints and audit-policy disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: final identity/ultimate-policy arbitration belongs to the user/operator or owning institution outside this packaged runtime. - Decision owner: external operator; configuration parsing, approvals and auditor dispositions are operational or quality controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: API tokens, allowlists, workspace containment, policy settings, audit-disposition records. - Closure path: an operator configures/approves a bounded run; no shipped constitutional-identity proposal → legitimate parent S5 decision → returned binding identity-policy change is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tools can block unauthorized operations and operators can resolve audit holds, but neither demonstrates that the runtime independently defines its legitimate purpose. - Evidence: pinned CLI documentation, campaign auditor and control modules above. - Basis: negative boundary and ownership test. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a larger deployment may implement a separate S5 parent; not inherited here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4e83884ec998546fd68ec77b106b7964cbe589dd/assessments/au/autocontext.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4e83884ec998546fd68ec77b106b7964cbe589dd\">4e83884 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T13:59:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/smartcomputer-ai/lightspeed\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lightspeed</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/smartcomputer-ai/lightspeed/tree/2873e77dca4325aac116b81db2b520502c037ac4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2873e77dca4325aac116b81db2b520502c037ac4\">2873e77 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Interpret incoming work and execute model-directed actions in a durable bot session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unpredictable external events, files and tool results, model/provider failures, tasks with open-ended next steps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select tools, read event details, invoke subagents or external actions and choose the event disposition. - Decision owner: First-party model-facing agent loop within each configured Lightspeed bot session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Event-sourced reducer, Temporal recovery, CAS references, tool registry, one-active-run admission, profile grants, tools and fixed budget controls. - Closure path: Event starts session run, model reads evidence and calls tools, effects are committed and returned for next model step; boteventresolve records the agent&#39;s decision. - Boundary reachability: The documented local stack starts session and bot workers; a created bot gets Main and configured routed sessions with model, standing brief and event tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The LLM chooses meaningful event actions and outcome, whereas the workflow merely preserves and executes their effects. - Evidence: core agent drive and durable loop, bot instructions/outcome tool, bot event guide. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: No external provider or deployed fleet was run in this source review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No function-specific agent-owned or constructor-complete inter-S1 conflict regulator is established for this bot&#39;s multiple sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Busy-session delivery, repeated events and load bursts are attenuated mechanically; no separately evidenced interference among distinct viable operational sessions is resolved. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A configured trigger picks queue/steer/append/coalesce, but this ordinarily changes admission to a target session rather than resolving a conflict among distinct S1 cells. - Decision owner: Operator/model can set trigger policies; deterministic bot controller dispatches and deduplicates, without a verified distinct inter-S1 conflict decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Event ids, CEL route keys, busy delivery modes, coalescing, rate/breaker limits, federation receipts and subagent limits. - Closure path: Buffered events are eventually delivered; a sender may get a receipt, but no source witness ties this ordinary traffic to a corrective decision between separately operating S1 cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A message bus, per-session queue, default scheduler and delegation are not alone S2, even though their safety/throughput roles are useful. - Evidence: controller lanes and state, event route policies, federation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A configured concrete multi-bot workplace may add inter-S1 interference regulation; generic interoperability is not counted.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Oversee the current standing bot&#39;s event/session portfolio, admission constraints, work intake and bot-level interventions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repeated failed events, flooded inbox, multiple active delivery threads, budget exhaustion and mismatched routing or work cadence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In selfConfig mode the Main model can inspect all current sessions, recent/active deliveries, buffers and run usage, then revise trigger routes, filters, cadence and busy-session policies. Human Operator can additionally alter whole-bot run budgets, pause/replay/reset and related current commitments. - Decision owner: Model in the enabled selfConfig Main mode (A); legitimate human Operator/Admin controlling the bot&#39;s full settings and Activity (P). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: botstatus, boteventlist/read, bottriggerput/delete, controller config signals, run budgets, breaker, session status and role-checked Platform actions. - Closure path: Current bot status exposes overloaded/failed work; model/operator revises trigger/control settings; API validates them; subsequent event admission and routed-session operation change. - Boundary reachability: Bot tools are declared in the shipped selfConfig mode and re-checked against the live grant; bot controller picks up configuration. Operator/Admin bot settings and Activity exist in shipped web/UI and core API. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model selects intake/priority constraints from whole-bot feedback in the autonomous mode; scheduler executes them. Separately, the authorized operator selects broader intervention and resource limits, so the parent mode is real rather than inferred from a pause button alone. - Whole-system current view: botstatus includes sessions, their busy states, active/recent deliveries, buffers, processed events, daily runs and breaker; Operator Activity supplies the same run/outcome context across the bot. - Current-control decision scope: Change which incoming events are filtered, routed, batched or queued and when future deliveries start; in parent mode also daily resource limit, pause, event replay and session reset. - Evidence: botstatus view, self-configuration tool declarations, tool execution and grant checks, operations and bot management, Operator role. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium for autonomous S3; high for parent bot management closure. - Caveats: The autonomous mode requires selfConfig granted by an Operator; the model cannot directly widen limits or reconfigure a different bot. Adjusting one ordinary session tool call is not S3.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No separate independent complementary S3 audit is wired to challenge ordinary bot operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect event handling, misleading tool results or missing outcomes can be observed, but no separate challenge authority is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Activity, session transcripts and event outcomes are accessible; ordinary user/agent review does not create an independent audit verdict. - Decision owner: Bot model for normal event resolution, operator for routine investigation; optional reviewer subagents remain delegated operational checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Event-sourced history, workflow logs, subagent transcripts, receipt records and event replay. - Closure path: Logs/status assist current troubleshooting; no independent sampled reality channel with a corrective audit judgment is guaranteed to reach S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Replay, normal QA and status dashboards are evidence-supporting infrastructure, not an independently governed S3 path. - Evidence: subagent reviews and limits, Activity/events, durable log. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A user may attach a truly separate auditor bot, but it is not part of the base bot boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Revise future environmental sensing and standing bot action opportunities in response to external observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Newly relevant external signals, stale polling/routing assumptions, changing schedules and information sources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A persistent bot model can read incoming external events and current trigger configuration, then select and install a new or revised schedule, poll, webhook, filter or route to govern future work. - Decision owner: Model in the first-party selfConfig bot mode; operator initially controls that grant and can override the setup. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: boteventread, bottriggerlist, bottriggerput/delete, polling cursors, schedule reconciliation, server-validated filters and controller. - Closure path: External event/evidence changes the model&#39;s judgment of what should be watched next; a prospective trigger option becomes persisted configuration and later yields changed work admission and present S3 control context. - Boundary reachability: SelfConfig bot tools are real admitted workflow bindings; puttrigger calls the core service and schedules are reconciled into the running controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic polling does not choose which future signals matter; the self-configured bot model can decide the changed future monitoring and enact it. - External distinction: New webhook, monitored HTTP/command output or peer event represents a changing outside system, not internal task-memory alone. - Future / prospective distinction: A rewritten future polling schedule, source/filter or alert routing applies to later events rather than completing only the present response. - Adaptation option generated: Model-authored new/modified watch trigger, cadence, filter and routing policy. - Path back into current capability / S3: bottriggerput persists and reconciles the trigger so subsequent event arrivals alter the S3 work portfolio and S1 sessions. - Evidence: bot trigger tools and descriptions, puttrigger implementation, polling/schedules and external feeds. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Requires an enabled selfConfig grant and a model choosing prospective changes; static operator-configured polling by itself would not be autonomous S4.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Retain legitimate human authority over the standing bot&#39;s job/identity, capability grants and final purpose changes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The bot&#39;s standing brief or authorized role may no longer match human institutional purpose or risk boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A human Operator/Admin can redefine the bot&#39;s standing job and authorized profile and self-configuration permissions after examining outcomes, including disallowing further self-revision. - Decision owner: Legitimate human Operator/Admin within the bot&#39;s universe; bot model cannot widen its own capabilities or override operator policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bot Job/Settings, role-gated bot document update, immutable bot ID, profile grants, live selfConfig and emit permission rechecks, controller idle-boundary reapplication. - Closure path: Operator recognizes a standing-purpose or authority conflict from the bot&#39;s Activity/conversation; revises Job brief/profile/grant; controller re-applies standing instructions and permissions; future operations follow the new identity decision. - Boundary reachability: Operator/Admin bot edits are admitted through role-checked Platform/core API and bot configuration reconciliation; subsequent Main sessions use the modified standing brief/profile/permissions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This credit is to the legitimate parent human&#39;s identity-level decision and its enforceable return, not to the existence of prompt text or an approval of one tool. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Whether this persistent bot should keep its ongoing job, change its standing instructions or retain self-configuration/federation authority in its universe. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Operator/Admin for the scoped bot and universe; no autonomous S5 right is claimed merely from botbriefput. - Return-to-operation path: Bot document/config update and session reconcile at idle change the instructions/grants for subsequent routed work. - Evidence: bot job and settings lifecycle, human permission hierarchy, bot document, controller config reconcile. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Routine message approval or role check is not S5; this parent mapping applies to a bot-wide standing mission/authority revision, not ordinary event handling or the entire platform.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b9b06fce8c71a0d48bccddb1a35972270a3b3792/assessments/li/lightspeed.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b9b06fce8c71a0d48bccddb1a35972270a3b3792\">b9b06fc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T11:17:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/peakflo/20x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">20x</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/peakflo/20x/tree/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01\">168693b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement real task artifacts through a chosen coding model in a task workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain requirements, source changes, failed commands and tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose actual file edits, tests, tool actions and correction attempts. - Decision owner: model-backed Claude/OpenCode/Codex/Cursor/Pi/ACP task worker, not the desktop scheduler. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent adapter, task prompt, MCP, worktree, transcript, checkpoint and status DB. - Closure path: task brief → model tool calls on repository → changed artifacts and evidence → task ready-for-review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the deterministic Electron app cannot choose code-specific corrections without the external model. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/agent-manager.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/adapters/coding-agent-adapter.ts - Basis: shipped task-agent execution path; external provider was not run. - Confidence: high for structural decision ownership, not measured outcome quality. - Caveats: human-only tasks and provider engine internals are outside the credited operating mode. - Boundary reachability: standard local task launch with a configured supported agent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no evidenced specific cross-worker interference repair in shipped task/subtask management. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two sibling tasks may duplicate or incompatibly edit a shared source interface. - Decisive decision or feedback right: parent model can inspect sibling state but no specific cross-S1 conflict judgment is required or returned. - Decision owner: parent task model owns decomposition; no qualifying inter-S1 coordination decision owner shown. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child status, worktree isolation, sibling summaries, wait/resume tools. - Closure path: subtask reports flow into parent prompt, without a demonstrated collision-specific correction loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: general instruction to coordinate and shared status do not establish actual inter-S1 variety damping. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/agent-manager.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/mcp-servers/task-management-core.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/worktree-manager.ts - Basis: bounded source negative across sibling prompt, subtask wait, worktree and parent notification. - Confidence: medium; models could voluntarily reconcile specific conflicts, but not proven as a shipped mode. - Caveats: no claim that sibling workers never coordinate in a user-specific deployment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current task-portfolio assignments and priority/resource fit under a selected model-backed Mastermind/triage mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing current tasks and uneven provider subscription quotas, skill fit and agent workloads. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses suitable agent, priority, skills, repository and children from live agent usage and task evidence. - Decision owner: triage/Mastermind model at current portfolio boundary; operator retains higher authority for risky commands. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listtasks, agent workload statistics, listagents usage limits, updatetask, createsubtask, start/stop tool gates. - Closure path: live portfolio and resource data → model allocation judgment → task DB assignment and priority → subsequent worker launch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fit and priority selection depend on a model comparing work and headroom; tools only execute the choices. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/agent-manager.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/mcp-servers/task-management-core.ts - Basis: first-party model triage instructions plus writable current task-management API. - Confidence: medium for selected on-demand whole-board mode; not continuous global executive autonomy. - Caveats: single-ticket delegation alone would not suffice; positive mapping uses portfolio-wide task and agent usage information. - Whole-system current view: Mastermind can list all tasks, current statuses, priorities and agent workloads; triage can compare all available agent headroom. - Current-control decision scope: reprioritize or reassign live tasks and pick resource/skill/repository commitments; no S4 strategy or S5 policy power. - Boundary reachability: desktop Mastermind/triage launches with built-in task-management MCP and model-backed session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge task ready-for-review claims against later real PR, CI and linked-issue state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new requested changes, failing CI or external issue drift after the task agent&#39;s last report. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate heartbeat model decides whether new external findings are actionable. - Decision owner: fresh heartbeat-specific model session, distinct from original task implementer session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: heartbeat.md, deterministic gh preflight, periodic timer, dedupe and authorization gate. - Closure path: task status/checklist → current external checks → model diagnoses drift → findings forwarded to task agent or to human for authorization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model interprets changed real-world state and requests correction; preflight and forwarding are deterministic controls. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/heartbeat-scheduler.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/agent-manager.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/shared/system-authority.ts - Basis: implemented separate session/current-state review and returned finding path; no live provider run. - Confidence: medium-high when opt-in monitoring is active. - Caveats: heartbeat requires a useful checklist and enabled task; this is no independent auditor organization. - Claim being audited: task agent&#39;s ready-for-review state implies no unresolved relevant linked PR/CI/issue updates. - Ordinary reporting path: task agent transcript, completion result and status. - Complementary access path: later model session polls current GitHub PR/CI/issue state against authored monitoring checklist. - Independence boundary: independent session and later external access, possibly same underlying provider. - Who acts on findings: task agent receives authorized corrective input; privileged actions are escalated to the human. - Boundary reachability: shipped HeartbeatScheduler auto-enables a new substantive heartbeat.md and runs for eligible ready-for-review tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no future external environment option-development loop is coupled to present S3 capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new tools and provider conditions plus changing user needs could require adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user chooses capabilities and model handles immediate task setup, not future strategy options. - Decision owner: human outside system for prospective strategy; no evidenced first-party S4 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: skills sync, user ratings, provider registry and usage statistics. - Closure path: retrospective feedback updates reusable instructions but not a prospective S4/S3 option agreement. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: auto-learning and present quota comparisons are not future-facing environmental intelligence. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/session-feedback.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/agent-manager.ts - Basis: bounded negative across feedback, skills, triage, usage and monitoring. - Confidence: medium-high for selected boundary. - Caveats: external operators can plan strategy independently of 20x.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity-level legitimate ultimate-policy adjudication is implemented within local task workflows. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission checkpoints, risky task steps and user approval conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user approves/rejects ordinary work and configures application settings. - Decision owner: local human owner for task approvals; no evidenced first-party S5 policy tribunal. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: checkpoint tools, heartbeat authority gate, settings and review UI. - Closure path: individual permission may unblock a task but does not establish an identity ruling returned to organizational policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a human action approval is not by itself a governing ultimate-policy exception. - Evidence: https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/shared/system-authority.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/mcp-servers/task-management-core.ts and https://github.com/peakflo/20x/blob/168693be23965f2e5cc8f15c32093544234c6d01/src/main/session-feedback.ts - Basis: bounded negative across checkpoint, permissions, feedback, configuration and task completion. - Confidence: medium-high within packaged client. - Caveats: owner&#39;s real company governance could provide S5 at another recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d9f102f34f602dcacbc9116de9fcc2e96dabeb4/assessments/20/20x.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d9f102f34f602dcacbc9116de9fcc2e96dabeb4\">2d9f102 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T22:51:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/orka-agents/orka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orka</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/orka-agents/orka/tree/7cffe130efa74f505d66dce561ade6c18901ba6e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7cffe130efa74f505d66dce561ade6c18901ba6e\">7cffe13 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: carry out user-directed AI tasks, generate code/reviews and use first-party registered tools to produce work artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous prompts, code/test state, model/tool results, errors and task-specific context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an instantiated AI worker or coordinator chooses content-bearing tool calls and task actions based on returned observations. - Decision owner: Orka-instantiated model-driven AI Task/coordinator; external coding CLIs keep their own lower-level agent loops and are not the sole witness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Kubernetes Task CRD, pod lifecycle, credentials, workspace, provider proxy, RBAC, task timeout and durable result store. - Closure path: user/Task prompt → worker/model decision → tool or delegated action → pod/run result → subsequent model decision or returned artifact. - Boundary reachability: README&#39;s in-cluster type: ai, chat request handling and registered create/execute tools are supported paths, not only demos. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-driven selection resolves task content; fixed pod orchestration and auth do not substitute for S1 discretion. - Evidence: README; chat.go; chattoolexecutor.go; general worker. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference among sibling specialist Tasks for a configured coordinator mission, including redundant race candidates and result-dependent message handoffs. - Distinct S1 units: separately instantiated coder, reviewer, tester or peer Tasks under one coordinator/transaction, each capable of independently performing work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: redundant simultaneously running alternatives consuming capacity, stale handoffs and sibling actions on incompatible intermediate results. - Attenuating coordination relation: the coordinator may select the first useful race result and cancel other running siblings; configured send/check message and explicit dependency handoff give sibling work a shared decision route instead of independent conflicting continuation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: waitfortasks returns terminal child status/retry metadata, canceltask stops no-longer-useful work and checkmessages changes a receiver&#39;s next step. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the witness is a selected collision/duplication-interference pattern with returned control into separately executing S1s, not generic parallelism or sequential tool calls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: race/redundancy and incoherent sibling outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which running child to retain or stop and when an intermediate handoff is safe. - Decision owner: model-driven coordinator only when a coordination-enabled Agent and corresponding task patterns are configured; primitives supply the constructor but no global automatic S2 is assumed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child identity, scoped task authorization, cancellation API, durable messaging and structured terminal waits. - Closure path: concurrent child observations → coordinator judgment about winner/dependency → cancel/message/wait invocation → sibling next behavior/terminal status. - Boundary reachability: coordinated execution and control are shipped as tools, not only example YAML; a configured coordinator can call them via the first-party session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent can make the interference choice in the composed race mode; generic messenger/Task storage alone do not supply a mandatory organization-wide coordinator, hence C rather than A. - Evidence: coordination section, registered delegate, wait, cancel and messages. - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current delegated mission, its specialist task status and correction loop, rather than all independent Kubernetes namespaces. - Whole-system current view: the configured coordinator observes the complete active mission&#39;s child-task status, rework, validation and review outcomes, not unaffiliated namespace workloads. - Current-control decision scope: current mission task assignment, retry, rework, cancellation, blocked disposition and completion readiness under the original user&#39;s authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failed specialist deliverables, validation/review failures, blocked Tasks and incomplete current mission steps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-driven coordinator decides retry/rework/delegation/cancellation from returned child/evaluation feedback and decides when to finalize or report a blocked mission. - Decision owner: configured coordinator agent; platform timeouts and allowlists remain subordinate enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: waitfortasks terminal returns, validation and PR-CI tools, task/agent creation and update, task records, bounded retry budgets in product guidance. - Closure path: mission plan → delegated work → wait/validate/review/CI feedback → coordinator correction/delegation → revised task outputs and terminal mission disposition. - Boundary reachability: createagent coordination and optional initialPrompt initiate a coordinator and subordinate task using the supported API, and the shipped coding workflow guidance explicitly loops over failed validation/reviews before PR/CI closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordinator interprets heterogeneous current results and chooses corrections, not just fixed scheduling; no all-installation budget/policy S3 is claimed. - Evidence: chatsystemprompt.go; delegatetask.go; waitfortasks.go. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the working PR/task outcome against exact-head evidence, then make findings consequential for mission readiness and rework. - Claim being audited: a coder&#39;s PR head satisfies acceptance, safety and correctness requirements and associated validation passed. - Ordinary reporting path: coder/implementation Task produces changed files, a PR/commit and self-reported success. - Complementary access path: separately configured read-only reviewer Agent receives exact PR head SHA plus bounded changed-file/patch context; the controller rejects stale or malformed verdicts and separately verifies required validation-task provenance. - Independence boundary: dedicated reviewer Task/runtime and read-intent workspace are isolated from the implementation Task, forbidden from modifying the checkout or receiving write credentials. Reviewer model may be an external supported runtime, but Orka owns dispatch, evidence identity, ingestion and return. - Who acts on findings: RepositoryMonitor records needschanges/safety states as blocked readiness; coordinator or explicit orka:fix/orka:fix-ci repair tasks must produce a new exact-head receipt, reset stale review and re-review before readiness. Optional automerge enforces fresh review/CI and policy gates; merge never follows review text alone. - Disturbance / variety regulated: optimistic self-report, untested diffs, stale review, hidden security/correctness defects, unsupported validation claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reviewer provides an evidence-backed contrary verdict and the monitoring/workflow path can block advancement or require repair. - Decision owner: distinct reviewer Agent for substantive critique; first-party controller for trustworthy exact-head admission and current readiness state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only workspace, signed/scoped inventory, JSON verdict schema, versioned review records, CI and separate validation provenance, bounded repair jobs. - Closure path: submitted PR → exact-head reviewer evidence → accepted verdict/blocked state → corrective agent task or human disposition → new head and re-review → readiness/merge policy. - Boundary reachability: the packaged reviewer Agent and RepositoryMonitor CRD/controller are first-party runnable paths, not a bare example prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the critique is a separate model-driven reviewer and its findings are returned through first-party readiness/repair feedback; deterministic SHA/CI checks enforce validity but do not generate the semantic review. - Evidence: RepositoryMonitor, controller, review result ingestion and repair. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct owner evidenced that interprets changing external prospects and selects longer-horizon adaptation of the Orka operating organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/provider changes, future project requirements and workflow evolution were checked as plausible paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no source-backed right to convert trend/forecast/strategy intelligence into a revised ongoing mission capacity/plan, distinct from present PR repair. - Decision owner: users/administrators preconfigure Agents, providers, schedules and policies; individual coding tasks may modify repositories but that is mission S1, not system-level S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scheduled monitors, task templates, runtime-policy config, CI and dashboards. - Closure path: recurring current-state review and operational corrections are evidenced, but not future-looking intelligence → adaptation choice → changed S3 capability → later effectiveness feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: repeated runs, CI repair, model configuration and repository improvement do not alone prove a distinct prospective adaptation owner. - Evidence: README; chatsystemprompt.go; RepositoryMonitor guidance. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no in-boundary unresolved ultimate-identity/policy adjudication and return process is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting goals, namespace trust and provider/tool allowances were considered but are preselected operational constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none evidenced for an independent identity-policy authority beyond fixed platform administration and task-scoped approvals. - Decision owner: cluster administrators and user/tenant policy configuration; automated admission/RBAC only enforce already chosen constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Kubernetes RBAC/admission, namespace authorization, scoped context tokens and provider/model allowlists. - Closure path: operator settings constrain later pods; no open organization identity-policy conflict → legitimate identity decision → authoritative return loop is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: project governance, role prompts, admission policy and security rules do not create a semantic S5 organ. - Evidence: README; security documentation; ToolExecutor authorization. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/10b55d8799d18dedfb18f8aaa4b1c20fd604d200/assessments/or/orka.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 10b55d8799d18dedfb18f8aaa4b1c20fd604d200\">10b55d8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T17:27:07+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hawkingrei/agenthub\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentHub</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hawkingrei/agenthub/tree/7f1f0450ebb58b3cb77b75c9669b4ad1ab8309f7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7f1f0450ebb58b3cb77b75c9669b4ad1ab8309f7\">7f1f045 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Workers deliver assigned engineering outcomes with local initiative and repair. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Implementation/test failures, new findings and scoped blockers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select local investigation/actions/repair and report evidence rather than wait for coordinator micro-approval. - Decision owner: Worker agent under the shipped worker execution role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ACP provider bridge, role-skill installation, task records, workspace tools and evidence stores. - Closure path: Task assignment/context reaches worker → agent acts → workerstatus/artifact pointers reach coordinator → accepted review or concrete change request drives continued work. - Boundary reachability: Team creation selects compiled worker prompt and role skill profile; ACP attaches mandatory managed skills before execution. External CLI is execution support, not an unexamined donor of the role’s rights. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Worker prompt explicitly gives initiative within its lane. Removing the agent leaves transport/task state without the same implementation decisions. - Evidence: skills/team/team-worker-executor.SKILL.md; crates/agenthub-team-prompts/prompts/defaultteamworkerprompt.txt; crates/agenthub-acp/src/teamroleskills.rs; src/api/teams.rs - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Prevent competing agents from simultaneously owning the same actionable mailbox topic and reconcile ownership/dependency conflicts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple workers can claim one topic or conflicting assignments; claim rejection identifies the active owner. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent chooses claim, watch/wait, release or peer/coordinator adjustment after concrete ownership feedback. - Decision owner: Participating Team agents; coordinator resolves cross-worker ambiguity when needed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SQLite topic claim/lease transaction, stable topic keys, mailbox error/status feedback and shipped mailbox discipline. - Closure path: A live conflicting claim returns ThreadClaimConflict/owner; losing agent switches to watch/wait rather than executing as a second owner; release/expiry enables later accepted work. - Boundary reachability: Both worker/coordinator ACP roles require the actor-mailbox skill. The shipped actor triage path invokes the same claim transaction and exposes its concrete conflict recovery; custom coordination code is not required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: SQL exclusion only enforces the selected claim. The mandatory agent mailbox contract supplies the behavior-changing triage response; a bare mailbox would not suffice. - Evidence: skills/team/team-actor-mailbox.SKILL.md; src/team/manager/mailboxthreads.rs; src/team/manager/mailboxerrors.rs; crates/agenthub-acp/src/teamroleskills.rs - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Source-based boundary judgment; no live model or production effectiveness claim. - Distinct S1 units: separate worker agents with assigned outcomes and local implementation discretion in the same Team. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing active ownership of one topic/task-related thread, not merely two agents exchanging messages. - Attenuating coordination relation: claim ownership plus agent watch/wait/release and explicit handoff. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: conflict response names owner and instructs loser to watch/release/wait, withholding duplicate execution until ownership permits it. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: source implements and the installed skill handles an actual exclusive-ownership collision, beyond message delivery.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Maintain cohesion of current Team work through explicit ownership, priority, accountability and intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stale/duplicated tasks, blocked workers, ownership mismatch, competing priorities and unsupported completion claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose/revise canonical assignee/priority and current task commitments, park blocked work, accept/reopen based on evidence. - Decision owner: Coordinator agent in the installed Team mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Actor task create/list/update tools, task journals, execution context, mailbox evidence, role/permission gates and runtime views. - Closure path: Coordinator reads current task/member evidence → prioritizes or reassigns/changes state with journal reason → persisted canonical task/actor message changes worker’s next lane → returned evidence is reconciled again. - Boundary reachability: Compiled coordinator prompt and mandatory role skills explicitly route to task governance/lifecycle; supported actor/API tools persist chosen priorities and assignees. No adjacent contributor-management agent is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This extends beyond decomposing one prompt: the role deliberately reconciles ongoing task truth, stale duplicates, ownership drift, waiting commitments and evidence freshness. Runtime SQL/permissions execute rather than choose these decisions. - Evidence: skills/team/team-coordinator-orchestrator.SKILL.md; skills/team/team-task-governance.SKILL.md; skills/team/team-task-lifecycle.SKILL.md; src/team/manager/taskcatalog.rs; src/team/manager/taskupdates.rs; src/api/teams.rs - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Source-based boundary judgment; no live model or production effectiveness claim. - Whole-system current view: Team task set/Kanban, member identity cards and checkpoints, blockers, notes and mailbox evidence. - Current-control decision scope: owner/priority/replanning, task commitment/acceptance and block/unblock intervention across the current Team.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No additional materially independent complementary operational-audit path established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Misleading execution or management claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separately authoritative audit judgment and corrective control return. - Decision owner: Coordinator reviews ordinary worker evidence; permission reviewer decides narrower execution permission. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ACP event history, task review, permissions and developer prompt-review plugin. - Closure path: Task inreview returns requested changes to worker, but this is the normal delivery/acceptance path; no separate complementary organizational-audit mode is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Architect/reviewer naming and raw logs alone do not turn normal coordinator acceptance into S3. - Evidence: skills/team/team-task-lifecycle.SKILL.md; src/team/permissionreview/dispatcher.rs; src/team/permissionreview/selection.rs; plugins/agenthub-prompt-engineering/skills/agenthub-team-prompt-review/SKILL.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party prospective environmental adaptation loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future capability/environment change beyond current task learning. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Develop/admit prospective organizational capability options from external distinctions. - Decision owner: Agents retain project lessons and may patch their own profile; user controls broader product evolution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workspace memory, profilepatchproposal, timed follow-ups and runtime recovery spine. - Closure path: Selected notes/profile changes inform later tasks, without an established external/future option-to-capability regulatory conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Self-profile drift repair and durable lessons do not alone establish S4; planned Mem/activation design cannot supply current closure. - Evidence: docs/features/team-workspace-memory-contract.md; src/api/teams.rs; skills/team/team-worker-executor.SKILL.md; README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No legitimate ultimate-policy/identity adjudication loop at Team recursion established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Purpose/ultimate-policy tension, beyond member identity metadata and execution risk. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Return an authoritative ultimate-purpose/policy decision into subsequent Team operation. - Decision owner: User configures Team; agents own only scoped self-profile proposals and operational commitments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent cards, role skill restrictions, profile patches and permission approvals. - Closure path: Description/prompt changes update local role metadata; ACP approvals permit scoped commands. Neither is an evidenced ultimate-policy decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The word identity in Agent Cards does not make role-metadata editing S5. - Evidence: src/api/teams.rs; skills/team/TEAMAGENTS.md; skills/team/team-task-governance.SKILL.md; src/team/permissionreview/dispatcher.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/16805b7f353f8cb58b1aafb55c1c3f20ef01076a/assessments/ag/agenthub.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 16805b7f353f8cb58b1aafb55c1c3f20ef01076a\">16805b7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T22:11:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Chorus-AIDLC/Chorus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Chorus</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Chorus-AIDLC/Chorus/tree/148b7928b99773f7fbdf61080940bd6995b06934\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 148b7928b99773f7fbdf61080940bd6995b06934\">148b792 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously implement bounded Chorus tasks and produce software changes/evidence under the task&#39;s acceptance criteria and project context. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository state, implementation choices, failures, tests, project constraints and task-specific ambiguity encountered while completing assigned work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool/debugging actions needed to satisfy an assigned task and decide when work is ready to submit for verification. - Decision owner: the autonomous task worker agent launched under the shipped Chorus develop/worker protocol. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task/proposal state, acceptance criteria, permissions, session/daemon dispatch, agent integration adapters, Chorus MCP tools and persisted work reports. - Closure path: orchestrator dispatches an unblocked task → worker reads Chorus context and executes autonomously in the target repository → worker reports work/self-check and submits for verification → resulting state/evidence returns to Chorus for audit/control. - Boundary reachability: Chorus ships the worker/develop skill contracts and supported adapters/daemon paths that launch autonomous coding-agent sessions as the standard operating surface; the external coding harness supplies execution capabilities but the Chorus project/task contract and return path are first-party. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the task contract constrains scope, while the running worker agent chooses the substantive implementation sequence and tool actions rather than Chorus deterministically prescribing them. - Evidence: README.md, $yolo task execution. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: S1 autonomy is credited to the running task-agent role reachable through Chorus&#39;s first-party integration surfaces, not to deterministic task-state machinery or to undocumented behavior of any particular external coding harness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material S2-specific regulation of a demonstrated interference/oscillation among distinct S1 workers is established at the declared project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as an S2 disturbance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as S2. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task DAG dependencies, unblocked-task queries, dependency-ordered waves, project/task state, comments and worker isolation organize and sequence work but do not by themselves establish an inter-S1 conflict/oscillation witness. - Closure path: dependency completion changes which tasks become runnable, but this is task sequencing/current control rather than evidence of a separate S2 attenuation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multiple workers and a DAG are insufficient under the Profile; the reviewed evidence does not identify a concrete recurring interference between S1 units and a distinct coordination relation targeted at attenuating that interference. - Evidence: $yolo wave execution, README.md. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: dependency ordering can prevent invalid execution order, but no separate S2-specific interference/attenuation relation is evidenced strongly enough to publish a positive state.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a project-wide current operational view and decide which commitments start, wait, reopen, advance or escalate. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete dependencies, failed or missing worker submissions, task-review failures, proposal-review failures, stuck work, exhausted review rounds and changing readiness across the task DAG. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next dependency-ready task wave, dispatch workers, decide how to handle non-ready/failed results, reopen failed tasks, advance verified tasks and stop/escalate when the project cannot progress. - Decision owner: the model-driven orchestrating agent executing the first-party $yolo workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent project/proposal/task state, chorusgetunblockedtasks, status transitions, acceptance criteria, admin transition APIs, reviewer comments, permissions and daemon/session lifecycle. - Closure path: orchestrator reads current project/task/review state → chooses wave/advance/reopen/escalate action → Chorus state/API transitions and worker dispatch implement it → changed task states alter subsequent readiness and execution → refreshed project state returns to the orchestrator. - Boundary reachability: $yolo is a shipped first-party Chorus skill and explicitly provides a complete autonomous mode using Chorus MCP/project state; its current-control decisions are not borrowed from repository CI or human project administration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-state and dependency code enforce constraints, but the orchestrating agent chooses the current portfolio action, handles exceptional results and decides whether to continue, rework or escalate. - Whole-system current view: project, proposal, task DAG, unblocked tasks, task statuses, review comments/verdicts and completion state across the Chorus project. - Current-control decision scope: current worker commitments, wave dispatch, rework/reopen, progression after review, and escalation when progress conditions cannot be satisfied. - Evidence: plugins/chorus/skills/yolo/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: server-side validation, permission checks and dependency computation are enforcement/support. S3 credit comes from the orchestrating agent&#39;s evidenced project-wide current-control choices.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge proposal and implementation completion claims through separate read-only reviewer agents and return blocking findings into corrective project operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missing requirements, weak acceptance criteria, dependency/design errors, implementation drift, circular or inadequate self-tests, build/test failure and developer self-verification blind spots. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect evidence independently, classify findings as blocking/non-blocking and issue the current round&#39;s VERDICT that determines whether the orchestrator advances or returns the work for correction. - Decision owner: the separate proposal-reviewer or task-reviewer agent for the corresponding audit stage. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only reviewer permissions, proposal/task/Idea/comment APIs, filesystem/test/build inspection, structured verdict format, persisted comments, review-round limits and admin state-transition APIs. - Closure path: producer submits proposal/task → distinct reviewer gathers complementary evidence and posts current-round verdict → orchestrator reads that verdict → FAIL causes reject/revise/resubmit or reopen/rework → the corrected artifact is reviewed again before advancement. - Boundary reachability: both reviewer definitions and the orchestration that invokes/consumes them are shipped first-party Chorus plugin surfaces in the standard autonomous workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewers make substantive adversarial judgments from separate evidence and are prevented from editing the work under review; deterministic state transitions only enforce the downstream response selected from the verdict. - Claim being audited: proposal completeness/alignment before implementation and task implementation/acceptance correctness before verification/completion. - Ordinary reporting path: PM/developer agents create proposal drafts or implementation, self-check/report work and submit it through ordinary Chorus state transitions. - Complementary access path: reviewer agents separately fetch originating Idea/proposal/task/AC/comments and inspect project files, diffs, tests/builds or proposal dependency coverage under read-only constraints. - Independence boundary: reviewer roles are separate spawned agents with explicit read-only restrictions and distinct reviewer contracts; they do not author the proposal/code they evaluate during the review pass. - Who acts on findings: the $yolo orchestrator reads the current verdict and rejects/reopens or approves/verifies accordingly, causing the affected planning/development agents to perform subsequent correction. - Evidence: proposal reviewer, task reviewer, $yolo review loops. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: reviewer verdict strings are not credited merely because they are called review; the positive state rests on separate evidence access, read-only independence and an explicit findings-to-rework closure.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then adaptation loop is established at the Chorus-project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S4 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project search, Idea elaboration, proposal generation, repository/context inspection, persistent reports and reviewer feedback improve current project execution but do not establish prospective environment sensing that changes the harness/project&#39;s future capability program. - Closure path: current-project information feeds current planning and rework; no separate future-facing adaptation selection/return loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning future tasks inside the current product request and learning from current review are not sufficient to map S4 without an environment-facing prospective adaptation loop. - Evidence: $yolo planning/review workflow, README.md. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Chorus can be extended and its agents can research externally, but generic agent capability or project planning does not establish S4 ownership for the harness at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the Chorus-project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S5 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as identity/ultimate policy. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user prompt/Idea intent, API-key permissions, admin approval/verification rights, project settings and review-round limits constrain operation but are operational/task policy rather than an evidenced S5 identity loop. - Closure path: permissions and approvals affect allowed current actions, but no disputed identity/ultimate-policy matter is routed to an authoritative S5 actor and returned as governing identity policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a user origin, admin permission or approval gate is not automatically S5; the reviewed project boundary does not expose an identity-level policy negotiation/closure path. - Evidence: README.md, $yolo prerequisite/admin flow. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: humans can operate/administer Chorus and configure permissions, but those facts alone do not establish a parent-governed S5 function under Methodology 0.3.5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/60fa7f6cbad5d00ad76aac4e43ee85735aaa106f/assessments/chorus.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 60fa7f6cbad5d00ad76aac4e43ee85735aaa106f\">60fa7f6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T21:13:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sd0xdev/sd0x-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">sd0x-dev-flow</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sd0xdev/sd0x-harness/tree/04e8a5e3f5d9c482937c2ad2170fab5fbd02b630\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 04e8a5e3f5d9c482937c2ad2170fab5fbd02b630\">04e8a5e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement and verify a requested software change or investigation in a user project through model-directed project work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar project context, failing tests, faulty edits, changed requirements and review findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose inspections, edits, tests, delegated specialist assistance and repairs in response to evidence. - Decision owner: model-driven Claude Code engineering actor using the installed first-party project rules and specialist skills; the external Claude host executes tool calls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plugin rules, installed project setup, hooks, review-state digest, git guards and verification scripts. - Closure path: user task → actor work and tools → test/review evidence or failure → actor correction/re-verification → project changes and report. - Boundary reachability: the documented full Claude plugin mode installs skills/rules/hooks and the session imports them; unlike skills-only mode, the full mode supplies the completion and review path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the actor selects engineering actions, whereas scripts record and gate outcomes without choosing code changes; external host internals are not credited as first-party decisions. - Evidence: installation and ownership model, active session rules, project setup and installed surfaces, hook wiring. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: source demonstrates a supported installed mode, not deployment frequency or measured task success; first-party credit is for the composed plugin mode, not the external host&#39;s general autonomous capabilities.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct inter-S1 interference regulation is established for the assessed standard coding session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: read-only fanout and specialist handoffs are present, but no concrete conflicting changes between autonomous S1 work cells are regulated by a specified returned coordination decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: not established; planner/executor role dispatch and report ordering do not themselves decide collision attenuation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: report-only workflow planning, bounded read-only fanout and sequential specialist skill calls. - Closure path: workflow reports return to the caller; no evidenced change to later competing S1 behaviour from conflict regulation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-driven orchestration of independent read-only findings alone is not S2. - Evidence: read-only orchestrate contract, single reviewer default, specialist definition. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: other user-composed multi-agent modes are possible but are not credited without a shipped interference-specific return loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-system current-control relation is established at the configured project-session boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risk severity, code/doc dirtiness and review failures are constrained by local quality gates, not shown as current resource bargaining across operational units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: risk tier and gate obligations operate on task/change evidence without an evidenced whole-system allocation or corrective control decision. - Decision owner: model and user choose execution/review details within static policy; digest-state scripts enforce/report. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Anchor Register, tier selection, review slots, hook reminders, scoped guards and human exits. - Closure path: review failures cause local work/review retries; no independently evidenced S3 current-control feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent-owned local repair and deterministic completion invariant do not themselves establish S3. - Evidence: review tiers and loop, policy tiers and ownership, digest-bound state, nonblocking hook. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: risk-scaled verification is important but cannot substitute for a whole-system current view.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge an implementer&#39;s completion/correctness claim by inspecting actual changed project artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a producer may falsely declare completion or miss faults not visible in its own summary/checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: give a separate severity-scoped audit verdict from raw code/diff access and determine whether corrective work is owed. - Decision owner: the separately dispatched Codex reviewer model makes the audit judgment; the producing Claude actor owns subsequent repairs; no digest parser is credited as auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only codex exec transport, frozen changed-file metadata, review-state verdict slot, risk thresholds and fallback validation. - Closure path: after code changes, /codex-review-fast → independent reviewer source investigation → Ready/Blocked findings → producing actor fixes blocking issues → re-review → fresh pass records a digest-bound slot required before completion. - Boundary reachability: the full plugin installs the review skill, transport and model-facing auto-loop obligations, and hooks remind after edits; the review is a documented first-party supported optional tool path, not a CI-only or developer-dogfood audit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a different model agent reads the actual diff/source and decides findings; transport, severity parser and receipt record do not substitute for its judgment. - Claim being audited: project change is correct enough under the configured risk tier to be complete. - Ordinary reporting path: producer&#39;s change summary, tests and its own verification. - Complementary access path: separate Codex sandbox source/diff inspection and independent review prompt; fallback reviewers are controlled by recorded transport failure semantics. - Independence boundary: read-only external Codex session receives frozen task and changed-file metadata but reads source itself; it is not the producer&#39;s self-verification. Both hosted tools remain outside first-party implementation ownership. - Who acts on findings: producing Claude actor performs scoped fixes and re-dispatches; human is reserved for explicitly enumerated exits. - Evidence: independent source review and fix loop, severity/scope gate, separate reviewer transport, repeat-until-current-pass contract, digest-bound return. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: evidence is shipped protocol/code, not a captured successful live independent audit; review may be blocked by missing external Codex credentials or human exits in particular installations.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: adapt subsequent project-engineering guidance from recurring external/user corrections. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated user-observed failures whose recurrence is not prevented by current rules. - Decisive decision or feedback right: distinguish a reusable error pattern and, after repetition, update project-local operational guidance for future sessions. - Decision owner: the model-driven engineering actor following the first-party self-improvement rule; the user can originate corrections and decide version-control inclusion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: namespaced lesson log, repeated-pattern criterion, rule-file update and normal doc review. - Closure path: user correction on a recurrent failure → actor writes a structured lesson with prevention signal → pattern reaches promotion criterion → actor promotes guidance to project rules → future sessions read updated rules and act differently. - Boundary reachability: the published self-improvement rule is installed via standard full-plugin project setup and imported by session guidance; the namespaced lesson file is provisioned on first occurrence and later local rules are loaded. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model must recognize the pattern, select the prevention and write/publish the operational rule; file persistence alone is not the adaptation judgment. - External distinction: user correction provides external feedback about unmet project expectations, not solely internal planning. - Future / prospective distinction: lesson/rule promotion addresses future recurrence across tasks and sessions. - Adaptation option generated: prevention rule and detection signal reusable for later work. - Path back into current capability / S3: project-installed rules govern later Claude sessions, closing adaptation into operational capability; no distinct S3 is claimed. - Evidence: correction-to-rule procedure, rule installation, project rule imports. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: contingent on actual recurring correction and session-level rule promotion; source does not establish measured improvement or autonomous change to the plugin&#39;s published universal anchors.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision-and-return loop is established in the supported project engineering session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: local configuration, security anchors, scope requests and approval/merge risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no in-boundary identity-level decision is returned through an established S5 channel. - Decision owner: developer and user set framework identity and non-negotiable rules; model may deviate from defaults only inside those constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fixed Anchor Register, project overrides, user confirmation and protected git/review gates. - Closure path: local tasks use preselected rules; no evidenced policy-level proposal → legitimate parent judgment → returned change to the session&#39;s identity/ultimate policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static constraint hierarchy and user approval of ordinary actions do not create S5 closure. - Evidence: Anchor/Default boundaries, fixed security policy, scope and developer role. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: project maintainers can change the published plugin policy in a different recursion, which is explicitly excluded here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d6959de776c5df5b41ae7fb77c94cfd9bd6236ee/assessments/sd/sd0x-dev-flow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d6959de776c5df5b41ae7fb77c94cfd9bd6236ee\">d6959de &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T21:37:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/coleam00/your-claude-engineer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Your Claude Engineer</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/coleam00/your-claude-engineer/tree/e7c064ac915cfeb29496c558795efa125a121863\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e7c064ac915cfeb29496c558795efa125a121863\">e7c064a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement a selected app feature through model-selected coding actions, browser tests and correction from tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete feature requirements, changing code context, failing browser flows, runtime errors and interrupted long-running model sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: coding model chooses concrete edits, commands, browser inspections and repairs from the orchestrator&#39;s issue context and observed tool outcomes. - Decision owner: model-backed coding subagent executed through Claude Agent SDK; the first-party harness supplies role/prompt/tool configuration and continuity, not the SDK model&#39;s entire general capability. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generated project directory, first-party subagent definitions, Arcade tools, sandbox, bash pretool hook and retry/session boundaries. - Closure path: app specification and Linear issue → orchestrator delegates detailed work → coding model inspects/edits/tests → Playwright or tool result → fix and verification → implementation evidence returned to orchestrator/Linear. - Boundary reachability: autonomousagentdemo.py invokes the first-party run loop; client.py wires AGENTDEFINITIONS and built-in/Playwright tools into the SDK client; coding prompt directs real code/test actions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: implementation and repair actions depend on model interpretation of evidence; the shell allowlist, iteration delay and state markers cannot make those decisions alone. - Evidence: entry/session loop, SDK client and tool wiring, specialized coding definition, coding prompt. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a live provider/Arcade/Playwright session was not run; SDK authentication and third-party success remain outside the evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct mechanism is shown for attenuating a concrete disturbance between independently viable, competing operational S1 coding units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Linear, coding, GitHub and Slack agents exchange messages and roles, but no competing source-changing S1 claims or resource-contention disturbance is evidenced in the default sequential one-issue flow. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific allocation, negotiated conflict-resolution or revised reciprocal S1 commitments. - Decision owner: none shown; orchestrator transfers context and orders specialized tasks, while SDK Task dispatch executes bounded child work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role separation, Linear statuses, one-issue sequencing, local generated-project git and task handoff summaries. - Closure path: orchestrator orders a single implementation issue and passes results to Linear/GitHub/Slack; this is cross-role task routing, not a source-backed interference-damping return between contending operational cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model-chosen next issue belongs to whole-current S3 at this recursion; it does not itself close S2 competition among independent parallel work units. - Evidence: explicit one-issue-at-a-time rule and context transfer, sequential feature workflow, role definitions. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: external SDK may support parallel Task calls, but this source does not demonstrate a distinct concrete S2 attenuation path for the shipped project workflow.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current whole project backlog, work priority and resumed progress across many feature issues/sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stale issue status, wrong next-work choice, incomplete previously Done features, session context resets and missing acceptance evidence. - Whole-system current view: the model orchestrator requests Linear META context, counts across Done/In Progress/Todo and full details of the next issue, and reads the generated project&#39;s current state before deciding its continuation. - Current-control decision scope: model selects whether to stop and repair regression or proceed to the next feature, delegates the issue and decides when to mark it Done based on required browser evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which pending portfolio issue should be worked next and overturn current progress when verification fails; issue transitions/continuation return to Linear and next fresh session. - Decision owner: first-party prompted model orchestrator with live Linear agent and coding-agent feedback; neither static CLI loop nor Linear SaaS is credited with the model&#39;s project-wide decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: META issue, .linearproject.json continuation marker, project progress summary, subagent tools, status counts, completion signal. - Closure path: fresh session reads project/Linear whole-current status → orchestrator asks for retest → if failed directs correction, else chooses next issue → dispatches code and evidence → updates Linear Done/next state → next session refreshes the picture. - Boundary reachability: the active client loads orchestratorprompt.md and the iteration loop recreates a client per session, so this is not an unused design diagram. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the prompt gives the model discretion to choose/reprioritize actual current work and act on regression findings; the deterministic loop only reopens sessions and recognizes the completion token. - Evidence: orchestrator continuation and priority/verification steps, whole-project completion decision, fresh-session state and rerun, live prompt registration. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: the whole here is the one generated app&#39;s issue portfolio, not all of an enterprise&#39;s projects; the Linear account&#39;s actual data/permissions were not accessed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: test prior completed-feature claims against actual running UI/interaction evidence before the S3 orchestrator takes new work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Linear may say Done while a previously working feature has regressed; summary or screenshot receipts can be stale or misleading. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-driven coding subagent runs a fresh browser verification for 1–2 completed features and can return FAIL plus screenshots; orchestrator must suspend new work and request regression fixing. - Decision owner: separately invoked coding verifier model for direct Playwright observations; orchestrator owns current feature plan and the fix order. This is the same coding role as producers, but a distinct subsequent Task invocation, not a third-party auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mandatory pre-work verification gate, browser navigation/click/snapshot/screenshot tools, structured PASS/FAIL report, no-Done-without-screenshot rule. - Closure path: prior Linear Done claim → new continuation requests browser retest → independent current app UI observations → FAIL evidence → orchestrator orders fix and re-test before new task → updated issue/current project status. - Boundary reachability: orchestrator prompt makes retest mandatory on every continuation/new feature; coding subagent prompt defines a dedicated Verification Test task; Playwright tools are registered in the normal SDK client. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model verifier decides whether the live UI corroborates the prior completion account; neither the prior producer&#39;s summary nor a deterministic status parser supplies this judgment. - Claim being audited: previously marked-Done project features still work and are fit to leave closed. - Ordinary reporting path: Linear Done status, prior implementation/test summary and saved project/meta issue context. - Complementary access path: fresh coding verifier goes to running app via Playwright, exercises 1–2 completed features, observes screenshots/browser state and reports PASS/FAIL. - Independence boundary: verifier is a fresh delegated Task/subagent context relative to prior iteration and S3 issue status, but reuses the &#39;coding&#39; role/provider and is not organizationally independent; that narrower evidence-based independence is the claim credited. - Who acts on findings: S3 orchestrator blocks next feature and asks coding agent to repair a reproduced regression; verification repeats before onward progress. - Evidence: mandatory verification and remedial gate, continuation&#39;s direct retest and failure return, coding agent browser verification protocol, tool registry. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: prompts specify behavior; no captured live regression was reproduced in this review. The same coding agent role is reused, so independence is bounded to fresh contextual sensing versus S3/earlier Done account, not external independent peer review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped organizational loop selects durable prospective changes to the harness&#39;s future capabilities from external environmental intelligence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user-specified app goals and task regressions are operations/current control, not an ongoing externally informed future adaptation mandate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none beyond issue-by-issue project implementation and user-edited prompts/spec/config. - Decision owner: user supplies app specification and permission settings; orchestrator chooses present feature priority but does not autonomously revise the harness&#39;s strategic operating design. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: appspec.txt, developer-editable prompt files, Linear backlog, META continuation memory and model configuration flags. - Closure path: spec/backlog → present coding and feature selection; no external change signal → strategic option → selected lasting future operating-capability shift is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: long-running context and future work items are ordinary S1/S3 continuity, not independent S4 environmental search/adaptation. - External distinction: third-party project requirements and Linear status are task inputs, not autonomous environmental foresight. - Future / prospective distinction: an unfinished backlog is a plan for task execution, not adaptation of the executing organization&#39;s future structure. - Evidence: project specification and prompt customization, current issue workflow, session continuity. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user can change the prompts or request a different application; that is external reconfiguration, not a built-in strategic return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party autonomous ultimate-policy or legitimate organizational identity owner closes an S5 constitutional return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool permission, command safety, OAuth authorization and product scope are externally pre-set restrictions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none for determining the project&#39;s ultimate legitimate purpose or amendments to binding identity/policy. - Decision owner: project owner, external credentials administrator and fixed script/security policies govern these matters. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude SDK sandbox, file tool allowlist, bash PreToolUse hook, Arcade OAuth, fixed appspec and allowed-command list. - Closure path: configuration constrains tool execution; no first-party deliberation over constitutional goals and downward reauthorization is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: enforcement of static user/configuration rights is not policy-identity formation. - Evidence: security and permission construction, SDK tool/hook invocation, first-party bash guard. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high within reviewed boundary. - Caveats: external user/organization policy is excluded from this system in focus.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/21315010a2fe15de7f6fd1c8c001cbedcbb865f7/assessments/yo/your-claude-engineer.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 21315010a2fe15de7f6fd1c8c001cbedcbb865f7\">2131501 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T18:54:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/affaan-m/ECC\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Everything Claude Code (ECC)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/affaan-m/ECC/tree/2b6e839771e53096d8451a213d40dc64ec8acac0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2b6e839771e53096d8451a213d40dc64ec8acac0\">2b6e839 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute assigned project work inside ECC-managed model-driven sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, repository state, tool/test observations, failures, intermediate artifacts and uncertainty about the next substantive action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose implementation/tool actions and iterate from observations until the assigned task completes or terminates. - Decision owner: the model-driven agent session launched/managed by ECC. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ECC2 lifecycle/store, worktrees, process launch, session identity, output capture, stop/resume and NanoClaw persistence where used. - Closure path: assigned task → ECC-managed model session → model-selected actions → repository/tool observations → further model actions → session result/state. - Boundary reachability: ECC2 start and the session manager directly launch supported model-driven agent sessions; NanoClaw is also shipped first-party. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ECC lifecycle code manages the process but does not choose its substantive next coding/tool action. - Evidence: ecc2/src/main.rs, ecc2/src/session/manager.rs, scripts/claw.js. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the launched host harness retains its own internal tool-loop implementation; its S2–S5 semantics are not imported.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concrete collision risk among parallel agent S1 units working on overlapping/coupled repository state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: same-file/line overlap and dependency/tree coupling that can make concurrent changes interfere. - Decisive decision or feedback right: compute right-of-way and an S2-specific hold/steer advisory; a return consumer must still apply that maneuver to the affected S1. - Decision owner: no autonomous owner is closed over the full loop; current right-of-way is deterministic and the agent-return actor is absent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: working-set extraction, dependency graph, overlap/dependency/tree risk channels, priority calculation, advisory feed and worktree isolation. - Closure path: parallel worksets → collision-risk calculation → hold/steer advisory → constructor gap at the return leg into S1 behavior. - Boundary reachability: the scanner and advisory/event contract are shipped first-party paths specifically designed around inter-agent collision; the missing TCAS hook prevents A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic right-of-way calculation is not autonomous organizational ownership; ECC exposes the S2-specific path but not its autonomous closure. - Evidence: docs/design/agent-proximity.md, docs/control-plane/VIEW-CONTRACT.md, docs/control-plane/TCAS-HOOK.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: TCAS-HOOK.md explicitly marks the return hook design-only; older design prose must not be read as proof that steering is already wired. - Distinct S1 units: separate ECC-managed model-driven sessions/worktrees acting concurrently on the same project. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping edits and dependency-coupled changes create concrete collision/merge risk between those sessions. - Attenuating coordination relation: ECC proximity computes pairwise risk, priority/right-of-way and a hold/steer maneuver advisory. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: ECC exposes the action-bearing advisory as the S2-specific return primitive, but the consumer that applies it before an edit remains to be composed; this is the constructor gap. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the relation exists specifically to reduce measured cross-agent code collision, with explicit pairwise risk and maneuver semantics rather than merely moving messages or tasks.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: regulate current team/session commitments, allocation, backlog pressure, intervention and worktree integration across an ECC-managed project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing active/stopped sessions, unread handoffs, idle/saturated delegates, backlog pressure, worktree readiness and current need to add/reuse/rebalance/stop capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose delegation/assignment/rebalance/stop/resume/merge actions from current team state. - Decision owner: constructor mode — autonomous owner must be composed; parent mode — the human operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session/message store, dashboard/team/status, delegate-selection scoring, backlog counts, worktree state and daemon/process lifecycle. - Closure path: current team/backlog/worktree view → operator or composed controller chooses intervention → ECC executes it → commitments/state change → refreshed view reflects the result. - Boundary reachability: ECC2 ships Delegate/Assign/AutoDispatch/CoordinateBacklog/Rebalance/Stop/Merge surfaces and current team/status views. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic assignment/maintenance can execute policy but does not constitute a model-owned whole-team discretionary judgment; the operator path is separately closed. - Evidence: ecc2/src/main.rs, ecc2/src/session/manager.rs, skills/team-agent-orchestration/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ECC2 is explicitly alpha; roadmap-only autonomous orchestration is not credited. - Whole-system current view: dashboard/session/team/coordination-status surfaces expose active sessions, delegate relationships, backlog/current state and worktree information at the project recursion. - Current-control decision scope: delegation/reuse/spawn, backlog redistribution, stop/resume, team intervention and worktree merge/integration.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently audit a candidate using complementary reviewer actors and return defects into corrective production work before shipping. - Disturbance / variety regulated: semantic defects, hallucinations, incompleteness, compliance violations and technical errors the producer may miss. - Decisive decision or feedback right: two isolated reviewers independently issue structured PASS/FAIL judgments; any failure blocks shipping and supplies issues to a fix cycle followed by fresh reviewers. - Decision owner: the two independent reviewer agents for audit judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: identical rubric/input contract, context isolation, structured verdicts, deterministic both-pass gate, bounded iterations and escalation. - Closure path: candidate → independent reviewers → findings → both-pass gate or fix issue set → revised candidate → fresh reviewers → ship or bounded escalation. - Boundary reachability: Santa Method is a shipped ECC skill intended for supported hosts with subagent capability and specifies the full reviewer/correction topology. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the semantic audit judgments come from separate model actors; deterministic code only aggregates their verdicts. - Evidence: skills/santa-method/SKILL.md, commands/santa-loop.md. - Basis: explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: strongest independence requires a supported host&#39;s separate subagent contexts; the documented inline fallback is weaker and is not the basis for A. - Claim being audited: the produced candidate&#39;s correctness/completeness/compliance against the original specification and rubric. - Ordinary reporting path: the producing agent&#39;s own output and deterministic build/test checks remain the normal production evidence. - Complementary access path: two fresh reviewer agents independently receive the same original specification, candidate and rubric outside the producer&#39;s context. - Independence boundary: reviewers are separate agent contexts/processes and do not share each other&#39;s assessments; fresh reviewer instances are required after fixes. - Who acts on findings: a fix/production agent receives the merged critical issues, revises the candidate and submits it to fresh review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: turn operational experience and corrections into persistent learned behaviors that alter later ECC-hosted sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated user corrections, error-resolution patterns, repeated workflows and project-specific versus general behavioral distinctions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: base mode — background Observer identifies pattern/content/scope and updates an instinct; parent mode — user selects a project instinct for promotion to global scope. - Decision owner: base mode — Observer/Haiku agent; parent mode — human user for explicit promotion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool-use observation hooks, project detection, observation logs, confidence scoring, observer schedule, instinct stores and SessionStart injection. - Closure path: operational evidence → observer pattern judgment → persistent instinct → later SessionStart injection → subsequent agent behavior changes; parent promotion can widen a selected instinct&#39;s future scope. - Boundary reachability: continuous-learning-v2 ships the hooks, observer agent/loop, instinct store/CLI and SessionStart injection; the background mode is explicitly supported when enabled. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic capture/thresholds support the loop, while the observer model decides the learned pattern; the learned result is then automatically returned into later context. - Evidence: skills/continuous-learning-v2/SKILL.md, skills/continuous-learning-v2/agents/observer.md, scripts/hooks/session-start.js. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: observer defaults to disabled and is not viable as a persistent background process on native Windows; the A claim is for the supported enabled mode. - External distinction: user corrections, observed failures/resolutions and repeated workflows/tool preferences captured from real prior sessions. - Future / prospective distinction: whether those observed patterns should become reusable behavior in subsequent sessions/projects rather than only solve the current task. - Adaptation option generated: evidence-backed project/global instincts with a trigger, action, scope and confidence; related instincts can additionally be evolved/promoted. - Path back into current capability / S3: qualifying instincts are automatically injected at later SessionStart, changing the behavioral context available to future S1 work; parent promotion can widen their scope.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the reviewed ECC project/runtime recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying identity/constitutional disturbance is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5 publication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: consent/safety boundaries, profiles, permissions, rules, security hooks, governance capture, learned preferences and operator configuration. - Closure path: no standard path is established from a genuine identity/ultimate-policy matter through authoritative S5 judgment back into governing whole-system operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ECC&#39;s policy surfaces regulate tasks/tools/review/security but do not establish an identity/constitutional authority organ at this recursion. - Evidence: README.md, skills/autonomous-agent-harness/SKILL.md, skills/continuous-learning-v2/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit absence after function-first review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream organization can impose an identity/constitution using ECC primitives; that separate system requires its own assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/feba4db663392fe60ec2b4e2f4f6633748c0c1a3/assessments/ecc.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit feba4db663392fe60ec2b4e2f4f6633748c0c1a3\">feba4db &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T11:23:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCode Orchestrator</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/tree/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2\">fdba1b1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform code, test and project changes using an actual OpenCode backend agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing source, ambiguous instructions, failing tests and tool feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select which files to inspect/edit, what tests to run and what fix satisfies the task. - Decision owner: model-backed Commander working directly or separately invoked Worker; plugin only routes tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OpenCode host, tool adapters, session registry, mission memory and verification checklist. - Closure path: user task → Commander/Worker model tool actions → code/tests/evidence → reviewed mission result. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves prompt/routing code but no task-specific implementation choice. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/commander.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/subagents/worker.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/tools/parallel/delegate-task.ts - Basis: shipped model agent definitions and runtime delegation path. - Confidence: high for supported mode; provider performance unmeasured. - Caveats: standalone question-only runs do not exhibit a coding operational cell. - Boundary reachability: published OpenCode 2 plugin adds Commander; Worker is a supported optional delegate.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no concrete first-party cross-operational conflict resolution circuit was established for parallel workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent worker file edits or interface changes may be incompatible when merged. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Commander assigns scopes/dependencies and can review results; no specific inter-S1 clash signal/repair relation shown. - Decision owner: Commander for current task delegation rather than a demonstrated S2-specific conflict decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parallel task manager, agentConcurrency, task result tool, scoped worker prompts and session IDs. - Closure path: workers report results to Commander but a collision-specific balancing signal and returned repair are not evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: file ownership guidance and queue limits prevent some overlap but delegation alone is not specific inter-S1 oscillation regulation. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/commander.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/core/agents/manager.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/tools/parallel/delegate-task.ts - Basis: bounded negative on parallel task lifecycle and Commander scope/dep guidance. - Confidence: medium; a real Commander could notice conflicts not proven as a repeatable shipped circuit. - Caveats: S3 whole-mission corrective work and S3 Reviewer findings are not automatically S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: direct a whole currently active project mission, allocate scoped model work and intervene when delegated commitments or verification stall. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfinished TODO leaves, dependencies, failed delegates, stagnant iterations and incomplete verification of overall goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Commander selects task decomposition, model delegation, dependency ordering, replanning, repair or final acceptance. - Decision owner: model-backed Commander with whole-mission goal and result view, not the mission-loop continuation hook. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mission TODO/status/sync issues, parallel task list/results, progress/stagnation guard, checklist and durable ledger. - Closure path: whole mission evidence and delegated status → Commander reassigns/replans or repairs → worker next action and updated TODO/verified completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses revised work and scope; deterministic retry/verification cannot produce design-specific corrective decisions. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/commander.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/core/loop/mission-loop-handler.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/prompts/common.ts - Basis: first-party model role and continuing mission control feedback. - Confidence: medium-high for selected active mission mode. - Caveats: a trivial question or single direct edit has no multi-cell S3 demonstrated; budget/loop enforcement alone is not ownership. - Whole-system current view: Commander sees entire user goal, all mission TODO leaves, child task status/results and aggregate verification requirements. - Current-control decision scope: assign Workers/Planner/Reviewer, choose serial dependency order, take over blocked work, re-plan and decide evidence-backed completion. - Boundary reachability: normal Commander and /task mode with optional delegatetask, task-list and result tools.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a Worker/Commander completion claim by checking actual project files and tests. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false success, broken interface contracts, untested regressions and incomplete TODO verification. - Decisive decision or feedback right: dedicated Reviewer model decides which checks to rerun and what findings block completion. - Decision owner: fresh role-specific Reviewer agent, not original implementer or deterministic checklist parser. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reviewer prompt and scope, actual source/tests, mission checklist, sync-issues and returned task result. - Closure path: implementation result → Reviewer source/test checks → evidenced pass/fail/uncovered area → Commander repair or verified TODO completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewer makes code-specific fault judgments rather than only checking a manifest flag. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/subagents/reviewer.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/prompts/common.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/core/loop/verification.ts - Basis: supported optional role-separated reviewer and executable evidence-return contract. - Confidence: medium-high for configured review; no paid model E2E validation. - Caveats: optional delegation, possibly same provider and no external institutional independence. - Claim being audited: Worker or Commander claims an implementation is complete and matches acceptance checks. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer result, task status and self-reported tests. - Complementary access path: separate Reviewer reads actual affected files, interfaces and executes project checks independently. - Independence boundary: distinct Reviewer invocation and independent direct source/tests, not a separate organization. - Who acts on findings: Commander updates scope or repairs; Reviewer may mark only verified assigned checklist leaves and log integration issues. - Boundary reachability: published plugin optional Reviewer subagent available via delegatetask.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no future external-environment adaptation loop into mission capability control was proven. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider changes, future market/user requirements and long-term technical alternatives. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Planner and Commander choose current technical steps, not enduring environmental adaptation options. - Decision owner: user/project leadership for long-horizon adaptation outside plugin. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planner, mission memory, persistent TODO and model tools. - Closure path: current planning updates task implementation but no external future options negotiation with S3 is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an implementation plan or research tool call alone is not future-facing S4. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/subagents/planner.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/core/knowledge/mission-memory.ts - Basis: negative path review of Planner, mission continuation and memory. - Confidence: medium-high at package boundary. - Caveats: a user can prompt strategic research, which does not demonstrate shipped S4 governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate identity-level policy decision loop is wired into the packaged OpenCode mission plugin. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user permission, mission replacement, project policy and final action boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human sets goals, stops or replaces missions, and approves operations as needed. - Decision owner: user/deployment parent for policy; plugin only enforces selected task permissions and state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stop/cancel commands, prompts, bounded concurrency, session replacement and verification gates. - Closure path: human can cancel or redirect a mission, but an identity-level exception is not routed to ultimate authority and returned as governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: system prompt constraints and user approvals over coding steps are not S5 identity closure. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/commander.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/core/loop/mission-loop.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/README.md - Basis: bounded negative across commands, safety, mission replacement, prompts and user rights. - Confidence: medium-high for installed plugin. - Caveats: maintainer governance or an external company policy engine is outside the system-in-focus.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3ac4ecb7a0a5204143934e584720845a924425ea/assessments/op/opencode-orchestrator.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3ac4ecb7a0a5204143934e584720845a924425ea\">3ac4ecb &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T00:24:53+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aden-hive/hive\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hive</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aden-hive/hive/tree/0c387492067e8b7d3e1c803009169f202f30ed77\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0c387492067e8b7d3e1c803009169f202f30ed77\">0c38749 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: Queen/workers autonomously perform outcome-producing business-process work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: the standard colony supplies agent-owned coordination through its shared ledger/plan, worker-report event path and Queen-driven convergence, providing concrete task-ownership and fleet-awareness mechanisms rather than simple one-shot delegation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the persistent Queen has a colony-wide current view through the plan/tracker and explicit authority to assign/fan out work, inspect and validate worker progress/results, converge execution and escalate exceptions. This is ongoing whole-colony current regulation rather than a component merely named manager.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: Queen-side validation uses the ordinary lead/control path and the human Sentinel is an escalation mechanism; the reviewed standard distribution does not establish a distinct sufficiently independent autonomous complementary-audit path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: reflexion, memory and learned skills improve operation over time, but learning ≠ a distinct external/prospective intelligence function coupled to S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: Queen personas, behavior triggers, HITL and cost controls shape operation, but legitimate ultimate task/policy authority remains with the user/configuration rather than an agent-owned S5 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/961323b8cd7cc12dfe8f927e44f355c2116c9944/assessments/hive.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 961323b8cd7cc12dfe8f927e44f355c2116c9944\">961323b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T23:12:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/1jehuang/jcode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">jcode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/1jehuang/jcode/tree/5f33d6239b56b3d381d6b2c0e9b151eef0b54cbe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5f33d6239b56b3d381d6b2c0e9b151eef0b54cbe\">5f33d62 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. jcode owns autonomous coding-agent sessions that execute repository work and can spawn/participate in swarm work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: A. The shared live plan carries explicit blockers, assignments and task state; worker outcomes and plan mutations change which nodes become runnable and which agent receives later work. Dependency and gate state therefore regulates inter-S1 ordering/conflict rather than merely routing messages.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: A. The coordinator owns the current shared plan and worker population, assigns/spawns work, observes progress/heartbeats and reclaims stranded assignments when workers die. This supplies autonomous whole-swarm current regulation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Gate/verify node kinds and artifact checks occur inside the normal swarm execution path; no materially independent reviewer/auditor with distinct access and corrective authority was established at the pin.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. Ambient Mode proactively inspects recent project activity and can launch useful maintenance work, while its memory learns from approvals/rejections. That is ongoing S1 work plus internal experiential memory, not a distinct outside-looking prospective loop that develops and feeds changed organizational capability into current regulation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. The safety/approval architecture at the pin is explicitly a design document; moreover action permission by itself is not identity/ultimate-policy closure. No implemented S5 tension-resolution loop was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/aa0f4fc244d04c276678f55ed941376422a344af/assessments/jcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit aa0f4fc244d04c276678f55ed941376422a344af\">aa0f4fc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T22:03:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AntigmaLabs/ante\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ante</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AntigmaLabs/ante/tree/74ce24554341fb0c6cc3521baa5da5b94cedf881\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 74ce24554341fb0c6cc3521baa5da5b94cedf881\">74ce245 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform user-directed software-engineering work through a repeated model/tool feedback loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous coding requests, repository state, file/shell/tool observations, execution failures, model/context limits and delegated-work results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive next actions/tools/delegation and decide when enough operational evidence exists to return a result. - Decision owner: the running root model agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: daemon/session turn lifecycle, tool implementations, permission engine, provider transport, persistence, compaction and external/local inference hosts. - Closure path: user task → model decision → first-party tool/delegation execution → returned observation → subsequent model decision → completed task/result. - Boundary reachability: the shipped Ante TUI/headless/server paths instantiate this model/tool loop directly; no downstream harness composition is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime mechanisms execute and constrain actions, but the model owns the substantive selection of what to do next and whether the task is operationally complete. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/reference/architecture.mdx, docs-site/docs/reference/tools-reference.mdx, README headless/TUI usage. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference may be external or local; ownership is credited to the model actor operating through Ante&#39;s first-party loop, not to the provider.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate contention among concurrently delegated Ante work units for bounded shared inference capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple child agents attempting concurrent model work when the selected local/model-serving environment can only process a smaller number of requests without queueing/timeouts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine how much concurrent subagent work the session may admit before excess work waits. - Decision owner: constructor/operator configuration; Ante supplies the function-specific concurrency mechanism but no autonomous agent owns or revises the cap in the documented standard mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxconcurrentsubagents, waiting-for-slot queueing and parent interruption/cancellation behavior. - Closure path: parallel child calls → cap check → excess child waits → slot becomes available → queued child starts; later S1 execution therefore changes according to the coordination result. - Boundary reachability: the cap is a documented built-in session setting applied directly to built-in Agent calls in ordinary Ante execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the root chooses which subagents to delegate to, but the specific collision-attenuation policy is selected through settings and enforced by the runtime; an autonomous S2 decision owner is not supplied. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/extend/subagents.mdx, docs-site/docs/configuration/preference.mdx, docs-site/docs/reference/tools-reference.mdx. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic delegation is not credited as S2; only the documented shared-capacity interference and cap/queue relation are used. - Distinct S1 units: the root organization can launch multiple built-in/custom subagents that independently execute real delegated tasks with their own model context and tool set. - Inter-S1 disturbance: child model requests can exceed the usable concurrent capacity of the selected inference environment; the docs specifically call out a local model that handles one request at a time. - Attenuating coordination relation: maxconcurrentsubagents limits admitted parallel children and queues excess calls until a slot opens. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: queued child work does not begin until capacity becomes available, preventing the competing S1 requests from simultaneously consuming the bounded model-serving channel. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation is explicitly tied to regulating a concrete concurrency/capacity interference among multiple operational child units, not to the existence of the Agent handoff itself.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current session&#39;s continuing commitment against a declared success condition and intervene when progress is insufficient or the goal is no longer reachable. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete work, premature completion, current-turn outcomes that do not yet satisfy the session goal, and situations where the active commitment has become unreachable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: after each turn, judge whether the whole-session goal is met, unreachable or requires another regulated continuation with corrective feedback. - Decision owner: the model-based goal evaluator in the first-party /goal mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: goal-session protocol state, deterministic continuation plumbing, session persistence and iteration cap. - Closure path: current conversation + goal → evaluator judgment → continue returns evaluator feedback into another operational turn, while met/unreachable terminates the commitment. - Boundary reachability: /goal is a shipped first-party TUI/protocol capability and can be used directly without a downstream controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the iteration cap is only a backstop; the substantive assessment of current performance and continue/stop state is made by the evaluator model and its feedback changes subsequent work. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/usage/goal-sessions.mdx, protocol documentation and changelog entries for goal evaluation. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary one-turn execution alone would not establish S3; this state is credited from the supported goal-driven mode. - Whole-system current view: the goal evaluator receives the active success condition and evaluates the conversation after each completed turn rather than judging one isolated tool call. - Current-control decision scope: continue the current commitment with corrective feedback, or end it as met/unreachable; this regulates current performance/accountability for the session as a whole.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently challenge changed-code quality claims through multiple review lenses and return complementary findings for corrective action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplication, unnecessary complexity, avoidable inefficiency and misplaced abstraction that can survive the ordinary implementation/check path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each independent reviewer judges the same scoped changes under a distinct audit lens and returns supported findings without editing the work being audited. - Decision owner: constructor path; first-party curated simplify supplies the audit organization and reviewer contracts, but the standard Ante installation does not equip/invoke this lane automatically. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: skill discovery/equipping, parallel subagent calls, shared review scope, prohibition on reviewer edits, parent deduplication/validation and subsequent normal tool execution. - Closure path: implementation/diff → independent reviewer findings → parent validates/deduplicates → worthwhile corrections are applied and verified. - Boundary reachability: the skill is shipped in the public Ante repository and is installable through the documented first-party skill mechanism; it is therefore a concrete first-party constructor surface, not a hypothetical downstream design. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewer judgments are agentic, but obtaining this S3 organization still requires the operator/developer to install/equip the curated skill; Ante does not supply it as a resident standard audit owner. - Evidence: curated/skills/simplify/SKILL.md, curated/README.md, docs-site/docs/extend/skills.mdx. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary tests, /diff, logs and root self-review are not credited; only the separate reviewer construction path supports C. - Claim being audited: that the current changed code is sufficiently reusable, simple, efficient and well-abstracted for the requested cleanup scope. - Ordinary reporting path: the root agent implements/inspects the task and runs its ordinary checks. - Complementary access path: independent subagents receive the same diff/scope under separate reuse, simplification, efficiency and abstraction lenses and return findings without modifying files. - Independence boundary: reviewer contexts are separate subagents and are prohibited from editing; the parent receives their findings only after each independent judgment is made. - Who acts on findings: the parent agent validates and deduplicates findings, applies worthwhile improvements and runs verification.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: learn reusable distinctions from user/project experience and convert them into persistent guidance that changes later sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurring user corrections, confirmations, project constraints, learned working patterns and memories that become stale or wrong over time. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which observed lessons should be recorded, updated or removed as persistent project memory for future work. - Decision owner: the running Ante agent under the first-party auto-memory behavior. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-project memory directory, typed memory format, MEMORY.md cap, session-start memory injection and Write/Edit tools. - Closure path: current user/project interaction → agent identifies reusable lesson → memory is created/updated/removed → future session injects the retained memory into the system prompt → later operational choices change. - Boundary reachability: auto-memory is a documented built-in Ante capability enabled by default in interactive TUI sessions and optionally in headless mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the user supplies environmental feedback, but the agent is explicitly taught to consult, record, revise and discard persistent memories; the runtime only stores and reloads the selected adaptation. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/extend/memory.mdx, preference/headless documentation. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: session transcript persistence alone is not credited; the witness is the agent-selected conversion of experience into reusable future-session guidance. - External distinction: corrections, confirmations and project-specific guidance arrive from the user/project environment rather than from an internal fixed task plan alone. - Future / prospective distinction: the agent evaluates whether a lesson should guide later conversations and can remove or update it when no longer valid. - Adaptation option generated: persist or revise a concise typed memory/rule describing how future work should be handled. - Path back into current capability / S3: retained memories are automatically injected into later session prompts, changing the options and constraints available to subsequent S1 work and goal-regulated sessions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop is established at the selected Ante boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operator-defined prompts, permissions, profiles and project settings constrain behavior, but no specific identity/ultimate-policy conflict or proposal is routed through an S5 closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision path with complete issue → legitimate authority → authoritative decision → returned governance was established. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user systemprompt/appendsystemprompt, permission modes/rules, profiles, project settings and generic AskUser questions. - Closure path: configuration is loaded into sessions and generic questions can return user input, but no function-specific S5 issue-and-return path is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parent/operator authority over configuration is real, but Methodology 0.3.5 does not treat editable policy text, permission gates or generic human input as S5 by themselves. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/configuration/preference.mdx, docs-site/docs/configuration/permission.mdx, protocol/AskUser documentation. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a wider organization embedding Ante may provide S5, but that authority is outside this standalone harness boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/85d7c52a6a7a091b79de156dc8d709c08773eff1/assessments/ante.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 85d7c52a6a7a091b79de156dc8d709c08773eff1\">85d7c52 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T22:13:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/coleam00/Linear-Coding-Agent-Harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Linear Coding Agent Harness</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/coleam00/Linear-Coding-Agent-Harness/tree/c37dc096993011cb0cc48a0afab176cd9c8f2375\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c37dc096993011cb0cc48a0afab176cd9c8f2375\">c37dc09 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: deliver working application features from a supplied specification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code, failed behavior, tool errors and incomplete features. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose implementation actions, tests, fixes and verified feature completion. - Decision owner: initializer/coding model agents in the concrete shipped SDK composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude SDK tool loop, local coding tools, Linear MCP, Puppeteer, Bash allowlist and session driver. - Closure path: agent observes file/tool/browser results, fixes the implementation, commits work and records issue completion with evidence for the next session. - Boundary reachability: runautonomousagent loads the shipped prompts and createclient tool configuration directly; custom agent organization is unnecessary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime continuation cannot implement a feature without agent choices; supplied requirements constrain outcomes rather than every action. - Evidence: agent.py, client.py, prompts/codingprompt.md, prompts/initializerprompt.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Static supported-path assessment; no live model/provider execution or effectiveness claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: avoid duplicate/conflicting feature ownership among project-working sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two coding agents acting on the same feature and interrupted-session work being mistaken for free work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: interpret In Progress ownership, avoid another actor&#39;s claimed work and publish a claim before implementation. - Decision owner: an autonomous coordinating/peer arrangement still needs composition when multiple instances operate; the shipped serial instance and status convention alone do not close authoritative concurrent ownership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Linear issue statuses, comments and per-session checks; no atomic compare-and-swap claim is asserted. - Closure path: claim/status and handoff become visible to later peer reads, changing issue eligibility; trustworthy attribution and concurrent conflict resolution remain composition obligations. - Boundary reachability: coding prompt Steps 2, 5, 6 and workflow prohibitions explicitly expose the collision-specific convention through configured Linear tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the function-specific feedback primitive exists, but multiple-instance authority/recovery is not fully wired; no S2=A claim. - Distinct S1 units: separately executing project feature sessions when the documented other-agent convention is used. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping work on an already claimed issue. - Attenuating coordination relation: publish In Progress and refrain from working another agent&#39;s In Progress issue. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: subsequent Linear status read directs the peer toward a different eligible issue or resumed own work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the cited rule explicitly regulates duplicate ownership rather than merely storing or routing tasks. - Evidence: prompts/codingprompt.md, client.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Serial default does not itself demonstrate concurrent regulation. The prompt also prioritizes interrupted In Progress work; distinguishing that from another active owner&#39;s claim is left to composition.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current commitments and project stability across the feature portfolio. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a previously reported completed feature regresses while new feature work competes for attention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reopen a completed feature, divert work to its repair before adding features and choose the next eligible priority from current project state. - Decision owner: current coding-session agent under the shipped project-wide role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Linear portfolio/status/META reads and writes, browser access and local tools; iteration cap merely bounds execution. - Closure path: directly observed regression causes Done→In Progress and repair; updated state/notes constrain subsequent feature selection and later sessions. - Boundary reachability: codingprompt Steps 2 and 4 run before Step 5; the concrete client supplies list/update/comment and raw app access in every normal continuation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent judges operational evidence and intervenes on project commitments; deterministic delay/cap does not make that judgment. - Whole-system current view: all project Done/Todo/In Progress counts, META progress, app specification, Git and sampled implemented behavior. - Current-control decision scope: project feature priorities, recovery of broken commitments and authority to suspend new feature development for repair. - Evidence: prompts/codingprompt.md, client.py, agent.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Static supported-path assessment; no live model/provider execution or effectiveness claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge predecessor session completion claims through complementary direct application inspection. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Linear Done/comments can remain positive while actual app behavior has regressed or was misreported. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose 1–2 previously Done core features, judge their current UI reality and invalidate a prior completion when contradicted. - Decision owner: fresh continuation coding agent, independent of the predecessor context being checked. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh SDK client, Linear claims, Puppeteer navigation/screenshots and issue update tools. - Closure path: a failed sampled check reopens the issue, records what broke and forces repair before new work; findings also enter META for later control. - Boundary reachability: the installed coding prompt mandates predecessor-feature checks before new implementation and client.py provides those browser/Linear tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the fresh agent makes the sampled audit judgment; tool output and status machinery only provide access and persistence. - Claim being audited: previous-session Done status and implementation/verification comments. - Ordinary reporting path: Linear issue statuses, implementation comments and META session summaries. - Complementary access path: direct browser operation and screenshots of selected already-completed core app features. - Independence boundary: fresh client/context checks predecessor claims without the predecessor&#39;s private conversation; same model family and serial role do not prove stronger institutional independence. - Who acts on findings: the current agent reopens/repairs and future sessions consume the changed portfolio. - Evidence: agent.py, prompts/codingprompt.md, client.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Credited only for the pre-work sample of predecessor Done claims. The same agent&#39;s Step 8 QA of its own new feature is ordinary production testing and is not additional S3. Static mandated path, not an observed audit run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: outside-and-then capability adaptation is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: only current application feature/defect requirements are regulated. - Decisive decision or feedback right: initializer planning and session pacing allocate existing application work, not prospective harness adaptation. - Decision owner: no function-specific first-party adaptation owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: app specification, META memory, feature priorities and browser feedback. - Closure path: no external/future distinctions→adaptation options→present harness capability return found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: project planning and bug recovery do not themselves close S4. - Evidence: prompts/initializerprompt.md, prompts/codingprompt.md, agent.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Static supported-path assessment; no live model/provider execution or effectiveness claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: identity/ultimate-policy closure is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operational task safety and supplied application scope, without a policy-dispute loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: static role, app spec and command permissions constrain execution; no ultimate-policy adjudication path is shipped. - Decision owner: user/developer sets initial requirements and configuration; no runtime S5 owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SDK permissions, sandbox configuration, allowlist hook and fixed prompt rules. - Closure path: no identity issue→legitimate authority→returned authoritative policy path found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: initial requirements and tool authorization are not runtime identity governance. - Evidence: client.py, security.py, prompts/codingprompt.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Static supported-path assessment; no live model/provider execution or effectiveness claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3b92b0844ae8313e11e6ade32409a9d1d9eca54a/assessments/li/linear-coding-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3b92b0844ae8313e11e6ade32409a9d1d9eca54a\">3b92b08 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T22:55:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/code-yeongyu/oh-my-openagent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">oh-my-openagent</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/code-yeongyu/oh-my-openagent/tree/879a8b791e511edee68bada80187e0febb9b1aac\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 879a8b791e511edee68bada80187e0febb9b1aac\">879a8b7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. Team members are built-in autonomous agents that claim assigned or available work and execute it in their own worktrees. Their model/tool loops produce the operational outcomes of the team.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: A. Team Mode closes a concrete coordination loop among distinct S1s: atomic task claims prevent duplicate ownership, dependency gates suppress premature work, isolated member worktrees attenuate concurrent file interference, and the lead/member agents autonomously react to blockers and task availability. This is more than a mailbox or delegation graph because the mechanisms change later worker behavior in response to shared interference state.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: A. The Team Lead has a whole-team view of tasks, messages and member status and is instructed to assign/reassign work, resolve blockers, manage member lifecycle, monitor progress and own project-level completion before synthesis. Those current-control decisions are made by a first-party autonomous lead agent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Specialist/reviewer-shaped agents exist, but the reviewed Team Mode evidence does not establish a materially independent audit channel with distinct access and corrective closure over the operating team.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. Research/delegation specialists gather information for current work; no distinct outside-looking prospective organizational adaptation loop was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. User prompts, agent definitions and team instructions provide operating goals and role constraints, not an ultimate organizational identity/policy tension-and-resolution loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9b431dfc4e818567d073e9a545bf346497b4addd/assessments/oh-my-openagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9b431dfc4e818567d073e9a545bf346497b4addd\">9b431df &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T20:14:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tale-project/tale\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tale</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tale-project/tale/tree/b2560c2d7d3294993e66129c891be4a059f3d6c4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b2560c2d7d3294993e66129c891be4a059f3d6c4\">b2560c2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce project task deliverables through agent-owned execution of assigned task briefs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous user requests, files and tool observations, provider responses, task feedback, source changes and execution failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: within an authorized turn, the configured autonomous agent chooses substantive task actions, tool interactions, corrections and the output to return. - Decision owner: configured model-driven project-agent actor in Tale&#39;s supported project-agent run; Tale does not claim first-party ownership of the selected external runtime&#39;s internal agent loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task assignment, task brief/context injection, sandbox session/worker, credential/tool bridge, output harvesting, run deadlines and agent retry. - Closure path: task brief and current project context → selected agent turn in its sandbox → model-driven actions/tool observations → updated actions and deliverables → persisted task report/artifacts and reviewer feedback → subsequent task work. - Boundary reachability: normal project-agent task starts create a standing sandbox turn for a configured supported harness; the implementation and task tools are shipped with Tale. No contributor-only executor is needed, though a configured external agent runtime/provider is a declared operational dependency. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the configured agent actor leaves task/sandbox/queue infrastructure but no substantive choice of task actions or deliverable; the host does not decide content. - Evidence: run host launch and settle; tool bridge task actions; README supported runtimes. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the supported composed agent-run path; no claim about autonomous management on a default unconfigured install. - Caveats: model providers and selectable external agent runtimes are dependencies. Their own S2-S5 are not inherited by Tale. A describes the reachable assembled S1 actor, not an autonomous Tale-owned model implementation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate operational interference between concurrent project-agent work cells through isolated workers, per-task single-live-run admission and capacity-aware waiting. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two attempted runs on the same task, multiple task turns sharing a worker&#39;s files, and agents competing for sandbox host execution capacity. - Distinct S1 units: separate project-agent task runs, potentially simultaneous on distinct workers/tasks. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping starts would collide on task ownership/review state; reused shared workspaces risk stale file contamination, while shared host capacity can cause execution contention. - Attenuating coordination relation: first-party task-run admission/status guards and worker-scoped workspace/delivery box, capacity queues and optional host heavy-phase leases. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: denied/already-running starts do not spawn another worker; queued starts wait for available capacity; a settled turn frees its worker slot, allowing later authorized work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the paths target explicit concurrent-run/cross-task file/host-contention disturbances, not merely task handoff or a mailbox. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide that conflicting starts should be refused or capacity-parked and that isolation/leases should govern subsequent starts. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic platform safety and admission rules; no Tale agent is established as owning a discretionary project-wide S2 coordination response in the credited base mode. This is a bounded S2-specific constructor/coordination path rather than A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: transactional run gates, standing worker allocation, task-specific output boxes, capacity retries and host lease tools. - Closure path: competing/oversubscribed start → guarded admission, isolated worker or capacity park → next start resumes only after resource/turn clearance. - Boundary reachability: ordinary shipped task-run/worker path; no extension-only coordinator is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: changing platform admission rules requires developer/operator composition; the configured work agent does not choose the S2 collision policy. C is credited to its concrete S2-specific path, not generic ability to send messages. - Evidence: standing worker isolation; task-specific delivery box; host lease admission; start tool occupancy semantics. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: mechanical isolation and exclusive-run rules do not imply that a model agent owns coordination discretion; host leases alone are not an organizational S2 decision.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current project-wide task priorities, assigned operational commitments and intervention/review handling across multiple agent work cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing unfinished tasks, changing assignments, blocked reviews, stale execution/review state and limited project-agent capacity. - Whole-system current view: project/board-wide taskfind lists task status, priority/assignee and captured review; taskget exposes task dependencies, current run and pending review. - Current-control decision scope: choose priority/agent assignment, start/resume another qualified agent, redirect a captured review to a different eligible independent agent, and decide which work receives next attention; operator task views/actions can exercise parent control separately. - Decisive decision or feedback right: project-wide assignment/priority and review-intervention choices based on current task portfolio, beyond one task&#39;s own model/tool steps. - Decision owner: base mode — explicitly granted manager agent with project-wide task authority; parent mode — legitimate project operator/editor acting through normal board/project task controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scoped tool grants, expected-value/CAS task mutations, current run identity, protected review transfer, capacity checks and status writes. - Closure path: manager&#39;s project task view → discretionary priority/assignment/review-transfer choice → guarded platform write → later agent starts/reviews use revised state; alternatively the human project operator chooses and commits an equivalent update through the UI/API. - Boundary reachability: Tale ships supported task-find/get/update/start/delegate-review tools for project agents; manager rights must be explicitly granted in the project. Direct operator task management is a first-party platform mode. Neither is a repository-maintainer or adjacent dogfood actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in manager mode, remove the manager&#39;s judgment and no project-wide choice of which task/agent/review to prioritize remains, only platform guards. In parent mode the operator owns that choice; neither platform lock nor scheduler is itself the decision owner. - Evidence: project-wide task observation; manager priority, assignment and review delegation; native task writes and actor paths; project task review and task UI. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for optional configured manager and project operator modes. - Caveats: having a manager label or an automatic scheduler alone is not evidence. The decisive right here is the explicitly scoped live manager&#39;s discretionary whole-project task-choice path. Ordinary operator approval of one output does not supply the S3 parent claim.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge an agent-produced task completion claim using source-bound artifacts and a distinct reviewer agent, returning a corrective or approving decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: misleading or stale self-reports, missing/inadequate output evidence, self-review conflicts, and acceptance claims unsupported by the actual task artifacts. - Claim being audited: the implementation agent&#39;s settled run and current task evidence support completion/acceptance. - Ordinary reporting path: implementation run&#39;s task comment and harvested deliverables enter inreview; the implementing agent cannot move its own task to done. - Complementary access path: a designated distinct review agent can read the captured source run, access available input/output files via guarded stagefile, perform independent checks and submit structured check/PR evidence. - Independence boundary: the reviewer must differ from implementationAgentId, must have the captured pending review/grant and matching evidence revision; human-competence and human-only gates cannot be bypassed. - Who acts on findings: the reviewer agent itself chooses approve/requestchanges. A serializable review transaction atomically moves the task to done or back to todo, posts feedback and records the decision; a separate implementation agent can then repair the task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judging the result of a separate operational run from independently accessed artifacts rather than accepting worker status. - Decision owner: authorized distinct reviewing model agent; platform validates source identity and commits the verdict but does not choose approve/requestchanges. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: source/evidence-revision CAS, staged files, structured checks, independent reviewer-ID guard, native approval record and audit log. - Closure path: settled worker run → captured evidence and independent reviewer access → reviewer verdict + explanation → guarded task status/feedback transaction → acceptance or a subsequent correction run. - Boundary reachability: a native first-party project-agent review mode is shipped; the reviewer must be separately granted the task-review tool and assigned an eligible captured review. This is not Tale OSS CI and not merely the worker&#39;s own routine tests. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the reviewing agent leaves a schema/permission gate but no independent verdict on artifacts. The worker cannot impersonate the independent reviewer; the model reviewer chooses the corrective judgment. - Evidence: captured source and distinct reviewer validation; native verdict and returned task status; review file staging and reviewer tool contract. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the supported independently assigned agent-review mode. - Caveats: the server cannot independently verify the substantive truth of every reviewer-supplied check, and native review is not necessarily a default path for every project. This positive finding relies on a separate authorized review agent examining actual evidence, not on the deterministic verdict parser or generic review label.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct external-and-prospective project capability adaptation loop is established beyond task work and current automation/operational controls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tale agents can research outside information for their assigned tasks and automation can schedule later runs, but these do not alone generate and return a project-level future capability strategy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no supported first-party S4-level prospective adaptation authority and return into capability is established. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task research/web tools, project history, automation editors/schedules, project instruction editing, agent/model configuration, recurring tasks and task-level planning. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model carrying out a foresight research task is S1 unless its result enters an established project capability-adaptation conversation. A schedule or prompt change does not itself close that distinct loop. - Evidence: task tool catalog; platform automations; project instructions. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: operators could compose future planning and adaptation tasks, but a hypothetical configured workflow is not a shipped independent S4 closure. No claim is made that future implementations cannot supply one.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: establish durable project-purpose and governing instructions that subsequent project-agent operation must obey. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project goal drift, incompatible instructions, unauthorized scope changes, and changes to the owner&#39;s project-level operating rules. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what the project is for, what instructions/constraints its agents operate under, and which rules are authoritative across future task runs. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: a legitimate authorized project owner/editor changes project instructions; a task worker or manager is not shown to possess a shipped autonomous right to redefine this project identity without separately granted parent authority. - Return-to-operation path: updated project instructions are persisted in the project record and read into the agent-run task context for later work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: approve and persist the project&#39;s authoritative durable instructions. - Decision owner: authorized parent project editor/operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project permissions, expected hash and audit logging, durable project table, mandatory context-injection helpers, run instruction snapshot. - Closure path: project identity/instruction change proposed → authorized editor chooses content → version-checked persistent update → later task-agent launch reads the project instructions and the agent&#39;s work is governed by them. - Boundary reachability: first-party supported project configuration/update operation and standard run preparation. No contributor governance or unshipped policy agent is credited. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent task tools can act within granted scope but do not by themselves select legitimate ultimate project-purpose changes; the parent editor holds the decision while the platform records and returns it. A task-level approval and static safety filter alone would not establish S5. - Evidence: authorized persistent project-instruction update; task-run instruction capture; project agent instructions. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this parent mode concerns project purpose/operating identity, not all platform-wide security policy or the Tale repository&#39;s maintainer decisions. No internal autonomous S5 decision path is credited.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3d84f95617c8dfaa9fad883bc20aaad7cbd9922a/assessments/ta/tale.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3d84f95617c8dfaa9fad883bc20aaad7cbd9922a\">3d84f95 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OpenDFM/Xcientist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Xcientist</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OpenDFM/Xcientist/tree/666eee404bc3cf59249dc8af0ab621cc3e804a49\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 666eee404bc3cf59249dc8af0ab621cc3e804a49\">666eee4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform environment-facing research work by constructing and executing an experiment project and iterating from observed execution/review feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing research proposals, incomplete project state, code/runtime failures, scientific-condition outcomes, artifacts and reviewer findings requiring context-sensitive work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose concrete code/project actions and repairs needed to satisfy a work-unit/phase contract and produce executable scientific artifacts. - Decision owner: the active first-party Experiment Agent worker/planner agent sessions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: phase runner, workspace/manifests, deterministic hooks, execution tools, phase contracts, artifact ledger and fixed master sequencing. - Closure path: proposal/work-unit context -&gt; agent chooses project/code action -&gt; runtime executes or writes against the workspace -&gt; execution/review evidence returns -&gt; agent revises or completes the work unit. - Boundary reachability: the Experiment Agent and its vendored harness are a documented shipped Xcientist CLI/pipeline surface, not a development-only example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic hooks and the master can block or transport state, but the context-sensitive scientific implementation/repair choice remains agent-owned. - Evidence: README.md, src/agents/experimentagent/README.md, src/agents/experimentagent/runtime/phaserunner.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination path is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple stacks/workers exist, but no specific inter-S1 interference, conflict or oscillation is identified and attenuated by a first-party coordination relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific discretionary coordination decision is established. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sequential pipeline handoffs, planner-to-worker delegation, phase ordering, manifests and shared workspace artifacts. - Closure path: these surfaces sequence/delegate work but do not close an interference -&gt; coordination response -&gt; changed subsequent S1 behaviour loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: worker/reviewer plurality and pipeline topology are not themselves evidence of S2. - Evidence: README.md, src/agents/experimentagent/agents/master/entry.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no agent-owned whole-system current-control function is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: phase readiness, incomplete evidence and failed checks are regulated, but through fixed rules/gates rather than discretionary whole-system current control over shared commitments/resources/priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the master computes RUNCODE, RUNSCIENCE or CONVERGED from fixed phase status conditions; no autonomous actor is shown revising organization-wide current commitments or priorities from a whole-system view. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic MasterAgent, phase reports, readiness flags, blocking issues and fixed code-then-science ordering. - Closure path: deterministic phase state -&gt; fixed gate decision -&gt; run/stop/advance; this is lifecycle enforcement, not a discretionary S3 control conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the master leaves no phase enforcement, but the organizational choice is encoded in fixed logic rather than owned by an autonomous agent. - Evidence: src/agents/experimentagent/agents/master/entry.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge ordinary Experiment Agent work claims before phase completion and return blocking findings into corrective execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: semantically wrong but superficially executable code, idea/protocol mismatch, scientific-invariant violations, weak integration evidence, reproducibility gaps and stale/unclean implementation paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue an independent PASS/FAIL judgment with blocking issues and required fixes after reading work artifacts and deterministic-hook evidence. - Decision owner: separate first-party read-only reviewer agents selected by reviewer role (ideaalignment, implementationcorrectness, scientificinvariants, protocolsemantics, integrationsmoke, reproducibility, codecleanliness, with analogous science review paths). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic hooks, review schemas, checked-artifact capture, immutable attempt reports, prefinish contracts and phase gate enforcement. - Closure path: worker claims completion -&gt; deterministic evidence is materialized -&gt; separate read-only reviewer inspects independent artifact/context surface -&gt; FAIL findings include requiredfix -&gt; same worker session receives the fix request and revises -&gt; reviewer/gate is rerun before completion. - Boundary reachability: reviewers and prefinish hooks are built into the shipped Experiment Agent phase runner and are required for phase completion, not benchmark-only code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: hooks enforce invariant checks, but the non-formal semantic review judgment is made by a distinct reviewer agent; the phase runner transports that judgment back to the worker. - Claim being audited: that the current experiment work unit correctly implements the idea/protocol and is sufficiently integrated/reproducible to advance. - Ordinary reporting path: worker final report plus normal execution/hook outputs. - Complementary access path: reviewer receives read-only access to the idea, shared review context, hook findings, worker report and relevant project artifacts and is instructed to inspect them independently. - Independence boundary: reviewer is a separate read-only agent invocation with a dedicated review role and cannot repair files; the audited worker owns implementation. - Who acts on findings: the Experiment Agent worker session, under phase-runner enforcement. - Evidence: src/agents/experimentagent/agents/code/reviewer.py, src/agents/experimentagent/runtime/phaserunner.py, src/agents/experimentagent/README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: sense external/future-relevant scientific distinctions, develop a research option, and return the selected option into present experimentation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing external literature, related-work structure, novelty/feasibility distinctions and alternative research hypotheses that should alter what the experiment organization attempts next. - Decisive decision or feedback right: synthesize surveyed literature/retrieval/memory into a structured proposal and choose/refine prospective research ideas through the Idea Agent&#39;s model-driven process. - Decision owner: first-party Survey/Idea agent stack, especially the Idea Agent (LigAgent) operating over survey-grounded retrieval and Memory-Guided MCTS. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: paper retrieval/clustering, graph-backed references, shared memory, structured idearesult.json handoff and pipeline runner. - Closure path: external literature/topic -&gt; Survey synthesis/retrieval state -&gt; Idea Agent generates/refines a prospective proposal -&gt; idearesult.json -&gt; Experiment Agent consumes the proposal and changes present scientific work. - Boundary reachability: Survey, Idea and the pipeline handoff are documented first-party CLI/runtime surfaces and are directly connected to Experiment in the shipped prototype pipeline. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: retrieval and pipeline serialization support the loop, but the prospective research-option synthesis/refinement is agent-owned. - External distinction: papers, topic clusters, graph-backed related work and other retrieved scientific context. - Future / prospective distinction: whether a candidate research direction is sufficiently differentiated/interesting and how it should be formulated for future experiment work. - Adaptation option generated: a structured research proposal/idea with experimental direction. - Path back into current capability / S3: pipeline handoff from Survey -&gt; Idea -&gt; Experiment causes the current Experiment Agent to build and run a project for the selected proposal. - Evidence: README.md, src/cli.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established for the Xcientist organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: topic, model/provider, experiment configuration and reviewer contracts constrain a run, but they do not constitute an identity-level conflict-resolution loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for identity/ultimate policy. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configuration files, research topic/idea inputs, phase contracts, reviewer policies and CLI parameters. - Closure path: configuration constrains execution directly; no identity/ultimate-policy issue -&gt; authoritative decision -&gt; return-to-operation loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no agent is shown owning the final authority to redefine or reconcile Xcientist&#39;s organizational identity/ultimate policy during operation. - Evidence: README.md, src/agents/experimentagent/agents/master/entry.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/xcientist.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-28T22:00:21Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/framerslab/agentos\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentOS</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/framerslab/agentos/tree/fa3ec4a47f8521026e8c10aa876d082152b289d8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fa3ec4a47f8521026e8c10aa876d082152b289d8\">fa3ec4a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute user/operator objectives through model-driven agent loops that select tools/delegations, consume returned observations and continue toward an operational result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: request ambiguity, intermediate model/tool results, external API/file/tool evidence, failures, and capability/role differences that change the next useful action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive model responses, tool calls, delegation targets/subtasks and follow-up actions within configured bounds. - Decision owner: the model-driven AgentOS agent; in hierarchical agency mode the manager model owns the substantive delegation sequence while delegated agents autonomously execute their assigned local work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, tool execution, bounded step counts, guardrails, schemas, retries, permissions, deterministic strategy plumbing and optional HITL constraints. - Closure path: request enters AgentOS → model selects response/tool/delegation → first-party runtime executes the selected path → observations/sub-agent outputs return into model context → model chooses subsequent action or completes → result returns to the user/environment. - Boundary reachability: ordinary agent/agency operation and hierarchical model-driven delegation are first-party documented runtime paths at the pinned revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic compilers and execution machinery transport/enforce decisions, but removing the model actor removes the substantive selection of tools, delegated work and response progression; the same decisions are not reproduced by static runtime rules. - Evidence: README.md; src/api/runtime/strategies/hierarchical.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: deterministic sequential/graph paths and lightweight helper modes also exist. A is based on reachable model-owned operating paths, not on every strategy transition being autonomous.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate substantive disagreement among distinct agent S1 contributions by making peer positions available for mutual adjustment and converging the resulting conflict into a coherent agency response. - Disturbance / variety regulated: different operational agents can produce incompatible positions, omit one another&#39;s evidence or continue talking past each other, leaving the agency with unresolved contradictory output. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each debate agent decides how to change its next argument in response to peer claims, and the final synthesizer decides how the competing positions are reconciled into one verdict/output. - Decision owner: distributed model-driven debate agents plus the model-driven synthesis agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic round sequencing, accumulated transcript construction, per-agent invocation, round caps and final synthesis prompt. - Closure path: multiple S1 agents produce positions → accumulated peer arguments are injected into later agent turns → agents autonomously rebut/refine in response → changed arguments enter subsequent rounds → synthesizer autonomously resolves the collected conflict into one agency result. - Boundary reachability: debate is a shipped first-party agency() strategy; callers select it directly without supplying their own coordination transport or reconciliation implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime determines when transcript material is passed, but it does not predetermine how an S1 responds to another S1&#39;s claim or which arguments ultimately prevail; those coordination judgments are model-owned. - Evidence: src/api/runtime/strategies/debate.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: generic graph dependencies, sequential routing, shared memory and message buses are not credited as S2. The positive mapping is specifically the debate-mode conflict/mutual-adjustment relation. - Distinct S1 units: two or more named agency agents with independent model calls/instructions that each produce an operational contribution to the same agency outcome. - Inter-S1 disturbance: materially conflicting or incomplete positions from different agents can leave the agency&#39;s response incoherent or blind to objections; the debate strategy deliberately surfaces this disagreement rather than assuming independent outputs compose safely. - Attenuating coordination relation: every non-opening debate turn receives the accumulated arguments from prior peer units and is instructed to attack the strongest opposing claim and add a new point; a final model-driven synthesis adjudicates the resulting positions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: peer arguments become input to later S1 model calls, directly changing what those agents consider and how they respond before final synthesis. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the transcript and round scheduler are only supporting channels; credit comes from their explicit use to expose and regulate inter-S1 disagreement through mutual rebuttal and reconciliation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: regulate current whole-runtime commitments and aggregate resource exposure through active-workflow admission and agency-level resource limits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: too many simultaneously active workflows or aggregate agency consumption can exceed configured current capacity/cost/time/call bounds and threaten the ability of the runtime to honor its existing commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose/revise whole-runtime concurrency and aggregate resource limits, together with the response policy when those limits are reached. - Decision owner: no autonomous first-party S3 actor owns those choices. The downstream host/operator supplies the control regime; first-party runtime components measure and enforce it. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WorkflowEngine.activeWorkflowCount, maxConcurrentWorkflows, workflow store/progress APIs, agency aggregate usage/call/duration accounting, checkLimits() and configured error/warn behavior. - Closure path: current workflow/agency load is observed → configured whole-system threshold is evaluated → excess new workflow commitment is rejected or a configured agency-limit response is raised → subsequent admitted work is constrained by that regime. - Boundary reachability: WorkflowEngine and agency controls are shipped first-party runtime surfaces and are directly configurable by an adopter without replacing the underlying control machinery. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime has real whole-system state and enforcement authority, but the organizational choice of concurrency/resource budget and limit-response policy is not made by an autonomous model actor. The function-specific decision path is therefore left for downstream construction, yielding C. - Evidence: src/orchestration/workflows/WorkflowEngine.ts; src/api/agency.ts. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: the hierarchical manager is not credited as S3 merely because it delegates work. Its documented scope is one-request task accomplishment. Likewise static limits do not become autonomous simply because the runtime enforces them. - Whole-system current view: active workflow count and workflow/progress state at the full WorkflowEngine level, plus aggregate agency usage, elapsed duration and agent-call counts for the agency-level control surface. - Current-control decision scope: admission of additional concurrent workflow commitments and aggregate agency resource/limit regime; the decisive policy values remain host-owned.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary audit path is established for the declared AgentOS operating boundary at the pinned revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: review-loop, standalone Evaluator/LLMJudge utilities, emergent capability judges, tests, tracing and provenance were inspected but do not close S3 for ordinary operations. - Closure path: no complementary operating-audit closure established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the review-loop reviewer consumes the producer&#39;s ordinary draft plus task and is part of the normal production QA path; standalone evaluator surfaces are not wired into current operational control; emergent judges audit proposed capabilities inside S4 adaptation rather than independently sampling ordinary S1/S3 reality. - Evidence: src/api/runtime/strategies/review-loop.ts; src/safety/evaluation/Evaluator.ts; docs/architecture/EMERGENTCAPABILITIES.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: detect externally presented capability gaps, develop and test new executable capability options, and return approved adaptations into the agent&#39;s future operating repertoire. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tasks and environmental demands can require a capability absent from the agent&#39;s currently registered tools, while candidate generated capabilities may be unsafe, incorrect or unreliable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model agent decides that a capability gap warrants forging and specifies a new capability; model-driven judging participates in deciding whether the candidate is safe/correct enough to enter the repertoire, within deterministic safety/test bounds. - Decision owner: the model-driven operating agent owns the adaptation proposal; the first-party LLM judge owns the semantic approval judgment for creation/promotion subject to hard runtime constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: forgetool, composable and sandbox builders, declared test cases, schema validation, hardened node:vm, EmergentJudge, EmergentToolRegistry, session/agent/shared tiers and deterministic usage/confidence thresholds. - Closure path: an external task exposes a missing capability → model invokes forgetool and proposes/composes implementation → first-party tests and LLM judge evaluate the option → approved capability is registered at session tier → subsequent turns can invoke it by name → sufficiently proven capabilities can move to a longer-lived agent tier, changing future operating capability. - Boundary reachability: the repository documents AgentOS.create({ emergent: true }) as a full-runtime first-party entry point that initializes emergent support and exposes forgetool; the positive claim does not rely on the lightweight agent() helper, which explicitly does not activate the path on its own. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic sandbox, schemas and thresholds constrain the search space, but they do not decide which missing capability to invent or semantically judge the candidate. Removing the model proposal/judge actors removes those adaptation decisions. - Evidence: docs/architecture/EMERGENTCAPABILITIES.md; src/cognition/emergent/ForgeToolMetaTool.ts; src/cognition/emergent/EmergentCapabilityEngine.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: emergent capability creation is opt-in rather than default. Opt-in does not negate boundary reachability because the full runtime ships the complete decision/feedback path. Generic memory/self-improvement alone is not used as the S4 witness. - External distinction: a task/environment presents a required operation for which the current capability catalog has no suitable tool. - Future / prospective distinction: the runtime does not merely improvise one answer; it creates a named executable capability intended for subsequent turns, tracks reliability and provides a path to longer-lived agent-tier reuse. - Adaptation option generated: a composed or sandboxed typed tool implementation with schemas and tests, proposed by the model and semantically reviewed before activation. - Path back into current capability / S3: approved tools enter the first-party EmergentToolRegistry and become callable by name on later turns; promotion can persist a proven capability at agent tier.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: establish and preserve agent identity, values, tone and hard behavioral limits through a durable parent-authored SOUL workspace that governs subsequent runtime behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/context drift or operational adaptation could otherwise make the agent&#39;s enduring identity, values and ultimate behavioral boundaries ambiguous across sessions and changing tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: define or revise the identity-level content of SOUL.md, including values and hard limits that bound subsequent operation. - Decision owner: the legitimate parent/operator who owns the agent workspace; no autonomous first-party actor is given ultimate authority to rewrite the SOUL identity/hard-limit source. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL workspace convention, YAML-to-IPersonaDefinition parsing, SoulLoader, system-message injection, structured persona fields and runtime prompt/persona machinery. - Closure path: identity/policy content is authored or revised by the parent in SOUL.md → first-party loader reads it at agent boot → structured identity/hard limits and prose enter runtime persona/system context → subsequent model operation is governed by that parent decision. - Boundary reachability: SOUL workspaces and souledAgent()/loadSoul are documented first-party runtime paths, with SOUL.md explicitly required for the souled identity mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: AgentOS can autonomously edit its memory wiki and can optionally adapt bounded personality parameters, but those lower-level adaptation paths do not transfer ultimate authority over the durable SOUL identity, values and hard limits away from the parent. - Evidence: docs/SOULFILES.md; src/cognition/substrate/personas/SoulLoader.ts; src/cognition/substrate/personas/SOUL.template.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: a generic system prompt alone would not establish S5. Credit is based on the repository&#39;s identity-specific SOUL boundary, explicit values/hard-limit semantics and closed load-back path. Bounded runtime personality adaptation is not treated as autonomous ultimate-policy authority. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: who the agent is, what values it embodies and which hard behavioral limits remain authoritative across changing tasks/sessions. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: parent-governed mode only — the workspace owner/operator authors the identity source; no autonomous S5 mode is claimed. - Return-to-operation path: SOUL.md is loaded at boot into structured persona fields and the leading system context, which directly governs later agent decisions and responses.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/db7e6211a5dcc311babd508512a358210aeb3c36/assessments/agentos.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit db7e6211a5dcc311babd508512a358210aeb3c36\">db7e621 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
